ems in language.\",\r\n      \"Morphology examines word structure and formation.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Pragmatics analyzes how context, speaker intent, and social factors influence meaning.\",\r\n      \"Semantics studies meaning but doesn't emphasize context as pragmatics does.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q128\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Functions in Context\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which sentence shows positive connotation?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"He's cheap.\",\r\n      \"He's thrifty.\",\r\n      \"He's stingy.\",\r\n      \"He's miserly.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"'Cheap' has negative connotations of low quality or excessive frugality.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'Thrifty' has positive connotations of wise money management.\",\r\n      \"'Stingy' has negative connotations of unwillingness to spend or share.\",\r\n      \"'Miserly' has strongly negative connotations of excessive hoarding.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q129\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Functions in Context\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Denotation refers to a word's…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Historical origin\",\r\n      \"Literal meaning\",\r\n      \"Figurative association\",\r\n      \"Emotional tone\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Etymology studies historical origins, not denotation.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Denotation is the dictionary definition or literal meaning of a word.\",\r\n      \"Figurative associations relate to connotation, not denotation.\",\r\n      \"Emotional tone is part of connotation, not denotation.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q130\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Effective Communication\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which is NOT a key component of communication?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Sender\",\r\n      \"Message\",\r\n      \"Grammar\",\r\n      \"Feedback\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The sender initiates communication.\",\r\n      \"The message is the content being communicated.\",\r\n      \"Correct! While grammar aids clarity, it's not one of the six essential components of the communication process.\",\r\n      \"Feedback allows the sender to gauge message reception.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q131\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Effective Communication\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which quality ensures effective communication?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Ambiguity\",\r\n      \"Clarity\",\r\n      \"Volume\",\r\n      \"Length\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Ambiguity obscures meaning and hinders communication.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Clarity ensures the message is understood as intended.\",\r\n      \"Volume is a delivery factor but doesn't guarantee effective communication.\",\r\n      \"Length doesn't ensure effectiveness; conciseness is often preferable.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q132\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Effective Communication\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Tone is defined as…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The emotional quality or attitude in language\",\r\n      \"The complexity of sentence structure\",\r\n      \"The literal meaning of a word\",\r\n      \"The rhythm of speech\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Tone reflects the speaker's or writer's attitude toward the subject and audience.\",\r\n      \"Sentence complexity relates to syntax, not tone.\",\r\n      \"Literal meaning is denotation, not tone.\",\r\n      \"Rhythm relates to prosody, not tone (though they can work together).\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q133\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Effective Communication\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Active listening means…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Hearing and ignoring irrelevant details\",\r\n      \"Responding thoughtfully and showing understanding\",\r\n      \"Taking notes only\",\r\n      \"Finishing sentences for the speaker\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Active listening requires engagement with all content, not selective ignoring.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Active listening involves full attention, comprehension, and meaningful response.\",\r\n      \"Note-taking is one strategy but doesn't encompass active listening.\",\r\n      \"Interrupting shows poor listening, not active engagement.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q134\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Effective Communication\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Nonverbal cues can…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Only distract from meaning\",\r\n      \"Reinforce or contradict verbal messages\",\r\n      \"Replace written communication\",\r\n      \"Eliminate misunderstanding entirely\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Nonverbal cues enhance and clarify, not just distract.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Nonverbal communication can support, emphasize, or contradict spoken words.\",\r\n      \"Written communication serves different purposes; nonverbal cues complement but don't replace it.\",\r\n      \"While helpful, nonverbal cues alone can't eliminate all misunderstanding.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q135\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Elements and Devices\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which is an example of situational irony?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The reader knows the killer's identity before the detective does.\",\r\n      \"A fire station burns down.\",\r\n      \"A repeated vowel sound in poetry.\",\r\n      \"The moral lesson of a story.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This is dramatic irony, where the audience knows more than the characters.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Situational irony occurs when the outcome contradicts expectations—a fire station should prevent fires, not burn.\",\r\n      \"This describes assonance, a sound device, not irony.\",\r\n      \"This describes theme or moral, not irony.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q136\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Elements and Devices\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Symbolism adds meaning by…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Simplifying description\",\r\n      \"Representing abstract ideas through concrete objects\",\r\n      \"Listing adjectives\",\r\n      \"Creating suspense\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Symbolism adds complexity, not simplification.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Symbols use tangible objects to represent abstract concepts, adding layers of meaning.\",\r\n      \"Listing adjectives is descriptive technique, not symbolism.\",\r\n      \"While symbols can contribute to atmosphere, their primary function is representing deeper meaning.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q137\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Elements and Devices\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A third-person limited narrator…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Knows all characters' thoughts\",\r\n      \"Only knows one character's perspective\",\r\n      \"Uses 'you' to address the reader\",\r\n      \"Is part of the story's action\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Knowing all characters' thoughts describes third-person omniscient.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Third-person limited provides insight into one character's mind while maintaining narrative distance.\",\r\n      \"Using 'you' describes second-person point of view.\",\r\n      \"Being part of the action describes first-person narration.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q138\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Elements and Devices\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Conflict in a story is…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The climax\",\r\n      \"The struggle between opposing forces\",\r\n      \"The exposition\",\r\n      \"The resolution\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The climax is the turning point, not the conflict itself.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Conflict is the central struggle driving the plot, whether internal or external.\",\r\n      \"Exposition provides background information.\",\r\n      \"Resolution shows how the conflict is settled.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q139\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Elements and Devices\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Theme is best described as…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The moral lesson or universal truth of the story\",\r\n      \"The plot's main event\",\r\n      \"The story's timeline\",\r\n      \"The setting's location\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Theme is the underlying message or insight about life that the work explores.\",\r\n      \"The main event is part of plot, not theme.\",\r\n      \"Timeline relates to plot structure.\",\r\n      \"Location is an element of setting, not theme.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q140\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Writing Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Diction refers to…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Sentence structure\",\r\n      \"Word choice\",\r\n      \"Figurative meaning\",\r\n      \"Dialogue pacing\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Sentence structure is syntax, not diction.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Diction is the writer's selection and use of specific words to create meaning and tone.\",\r\n      \"Figurative meaning involves literary devices like metaphor.\",\r\n      \"Dialogue pacing relates to narrative structure.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q141\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Writing Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which sentence best shows imagery?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"He walked slowly.\",\r\n      \"The sunlight spilled like honey across the table.\",\r\n      \"She moved quickly.\",\r\n      \"It was late.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This is simple description without sensory detail.\",\r\n      \"Correct! This creates vivid sensory experience through visual and metaphorical language.\",\r\n      \"This states action without engaging the senses.\",\r\n      \"This provides information without sensory detail.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q142\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Writing Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An author's voice reflects…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The plot's complexity\",\r\n      \"The writer's individual style and tone\",\r\n      \"The story's setting\",\r\n      \"The genre of the work\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Plot complexity is a structural choice, not voice.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Voice is the distinctive personality and perspective that comes through the author's stylistic choices.\",\r\n      \"Setting is a story element, not voice.\",\r\n      \"Genre is a category; voice is individual to the writer.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q143\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Writing Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Syntax primarily affects…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Sentence rhythm and emphasis\",\r\n      \"Theme development\",\r\n      \"Characterization\",\r\n      \"Symbolism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Syntax—the arrangement of words and phrases—shapes how sentences flow and what gets emphasized.\",\r\n      \"Theme emerges from content and meaning, not sentence structure.\",\r\n      \"Characterization involves personality development, not syntax.\",\r\n      \"Symbolism uses objects for meaning, unrelated to syntax.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q144\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Writing Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Tone in writing reveals…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The author's attitude\",\r\n      \"The story's location\",\r\n      \"The text's theme\",\r\n      \"The rhythm of language\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Tone conveys the author's attitude toward the subject and audience through stylistic choices.\",\r\n      \"Location is setting, not tone.\",\r\n      \"Theme is the underlying message; tone is how it's expressed.\",\r\n      \"Rhythm relates to syntax and sound patterns.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q145\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which best defines theme?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Setting of the story\",\r\n      \"Sequence of events\",\r\n      \"Central message or universal idea\",\r\n      \"Character's motivation\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Setting provides the time and place, not the theme.\",\r\n      \"Sequence of events describes plot, not theme.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Theme is the underlying message or universal truth explored in the work.\",\r\n      \"Motivation drives character actions but isn't the theme.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q146\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Characterization is developed through…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Dialogue, actions, and description\",\r\n      \"Setting only\",\r\n      \"Sentence structure\",\r\n      \"Theme repetition\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Authors reveal character through what characters say, do, think, and how they're described.\",\r\n      \"Setting provides context but doesn't develop character.\",\r\n      \"Sentence structure affects style, not characterization.\",\r\n      \"Theme repetition reinforces meaning, not character development.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q147\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which is a recurring symbol or image that reinforces meaning?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Motif\",\r\n      \"Plot\",\r\n      \"Tone\",\r\n      \"Foreshadowing\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! A motif is a repeated element that develops and reinforces the theme.\",\r\n      \"Plot is the sequence of events, not a recurring symbol.\",\r\n      \"Tone is the author's attitude, not a recurring element.\",\r\n      \"Foreshadowing hints at future events but isn't necessarily recurring.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q148\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Tone reveals the author's…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Mood\",\r\n      \"Attitude\",\r\n      \"Characterization\",\r\n      \"Diction\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Mood is the emotional atmosphere for readers, not the author's attitude.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Tone expresses the author's attitude toward the subject and audience.\",\r\n      \"Characterization develops characters, not tone.\",\r\n      \"Diction (word choice) creates tone but isn't what tone reveals.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q149\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Mood affects the…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Reader's emotions\",\r\n      \"Author's perspective\",\r\n      \"Character's motivation\",\r\n      \"Sentence rhythm\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Mood creates the emotional atmosphere that influences how readers feel.\",\r\n      \"Author's perspective relates to tone, not mood.\",\r\n      \"Character motivation drives plot, not mood.\",\r\n      \"Rhythm is a stylistic element, distinct from mood.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q150\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Imagery appeals to…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Grammar rules\",\r\n      \"The five senses\",\r\n      \"Symbolism\",\r\n      \"Syntax\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Grammar provides structure, not sensory experience.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Imagery uses descriptive language that engages sight, sound, touch, taste, and smell.\",\r\n      \"Symbolism uses objects for meaning; imagery creates sensory experience.\",\r\n      \"Syntax is sentence structure, not sensory description.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q151\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An allegory contains…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Only figurative language\",\r\n      \"Both literal and symbolic meanings\",\r\n      \"One simple theme\",\r\n      \"Historical facts only\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Allegory uses multiple literary devices, not just figurative language.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Allegory works on two levels: the surface story and its deeper symbolic meaning.\",\r\n      \"Allegories often explore complex, multi-layered themes.\",\r\n      \"While some allegories reference history, they're primarily symbolic narratives.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q152\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Foreshadowing helps to…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Create suspense and anticipation\",\r\n      \"Summarize past events\",\r\n      \"Reveal the moral\",\r\n      \"Confuse readers intentionally\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Foreshadowing plants clues that build anticipation for future events.\",\r\n      \"Summarizing past events is exposition or flashback, not foreshadowing.\",\r\n      \"Revealing morals is the function of theme or resolution.\",\r\n      \"Good foreshadowing enhances understanding, not confusion.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q153\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Conflict in literature functions to…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Stall character development\",\r\n      \"Create action and reveal growth\",\r\n      \"Describe setting\",\r\n      \"Provide exposition\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Conflict drives development, not stalls it.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Conflict generates plot tension and forces characters to change and grow.\",\r\n      \"Setting description is separate from conflict's function.\",\r\n      \"Exposition provides background; conflict creates tension.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q154\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Diction influences…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The story's rhyme\",\r\n      \"Tone and meaning\",\r\n      \"Theme repetition\",\r\n      \"Sentence count\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Rhyme relates to sound patterns in poetry, not diction's primary function.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Word choice (diction) shapes both the tone and the precise meaning of the text.\",\r\n      \"Motif creates repetition, not diction itself.\",\r\n      \"Sentence count is unrelated to word choice.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q155\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which is an example of verbal irony?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The opposite of what's said is meant\",\r\n      \"A surprise ending\",\r\n      \"Repetition of vowel sounds\",\r\n      \"A symbol representing an idea\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Verbal irony occurs when someone says the opposite of what they actually mean (like sarcasm).\",\r\n      \"A surprise ending may involve situational irony, not verbal.\",\r\n      \"Repetition of vowel sounds is assonance, a sound device.\",\r\n      \"This describes symbolism, not irony.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q156\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Symbolism helps readers…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Understand literal events only\",\r\n      \"Find deeper meaning in concrete details\",\r\n      \"Track dialogue\",\r\n      \"Identify tone shifts\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Symbolism adds layers beyond literal meaning.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Symbols use concrete objects to represent abstract ideas and deeper themes.\",\r\n      \"Dialogue tracking is a separate reading skill.\",\r\n      \"Tone shifts are identified through diction and style changes.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q157\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Plot is best defined as…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The message of the story\",\r\n      \"The sequence of related events\",\r\n      \"The setting's location\",\r\n      \"The author's word choice\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The message is theme, not plot.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Plot is the connected series of events that make up the narrative.\",\r\n      \"Location is an element of setting.\",\r\n      \"Word choice is diction.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q158\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Literary Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The climax of a story is…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The turning point or moment of greatest tension\",\r\n      \"The introduction\",\r\n      \"The falling action\",\r\n      \"The resolution\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The climax is the story's peak moment where the main conflict reaches its highest intensity.\",\r\n      \"The introduction is the exposition, beginning the story.\",\r\n      \"Falling action follows the climax, leading to resolution.\",\r\n      \"Resolution concludes the story after the climax.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q159\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which is an example of formative assessment?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Final exam\",\r\n      \"Weekly quiz\",\r\n      \"Report card grade\",\r\n      \"Standardized test\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Final exams are summative, measuring achievement at the end.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Weekly quizzes provide ongoing feedback to guide instruction during the learning process.\",\r\n      \"Report cards are summative evaluations.\",\r\n      \"Standardized tests are summative measures of overall achievement.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q160\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Scaffolding helps students by…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Increasing independent learning through gradual support removal\",\r\n      \"Assigning more homework\",\r\n      \"Memorizing vocabulary lists\",\r\n      \"Focusing on spelling drills only\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Scaffolding provides temporary support that's gradually withdrawn as students gain competence.\",\r\n      \"More homework doesn't necessarily provide structured support.\",\r\n      \"Memorization is one strategy, not scaffolding.\",\r\n      \"Spelling drills are specific practice, not the scaffolding concept.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q161\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A rubric ensures…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Subjective grading\",\r\n      \"Consistent and transparent evaluation\",\r\n      \"Faster essay grading only\",\r\n      \"Less teacher involvement\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Rubrics reduce subjectivity by providing clear criteria.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Rubrics define expectations and standards, ensuring fair and consistent assessment.\",\r\n      \"While rubrics may speed grading, their main purpose is consistency and clarity.\",\r\n      \"Rubrics require thoughtful teacher design and application.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q162\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Fluency in reading includes…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Memorizing text\",\r\n      \"Reading with accuracy, speed, and expression\",\r\n      \"Quiet reading only\",\r\n      \"Summarizing content\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Fluency involves decoding, not memorization.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Fluent readers demonstrate accuracy, appropriate rate, and prosody (expression).\",\r\n      \"Fluency applies to both oral and silent reading.\",\r\n      \"Summarizing demonstrates comprehension, not fluency.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q163\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which stage comes first in the writing process?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Revising\",\r\n      \"Editing\",\r\n      \"Prewriting\",\r\n      \"Publishing\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Revising comes after drafting.\",\r\n      \"Editing comes near the end of the process.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Prewriting includes brainstorming, planning, and organizing ideas before drafting.\",\r\n      \"Publishing is the final stage.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q164\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Metacognition involves…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Word memorization\",\r\n      \"Monitoring one's own understanding and strategy use\",\r\n      \"Copying notes\",\r\n      \"Listening silently\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Memorization is a learning strategy, not metacognition.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Metacognition is awareness and regulation of one's own thinking processes.\",\r\n      \"Copying is a mechanical task without reflective thinking.\",\r\n      \"Passive listening doesn't involve metacognitive awareness.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q165\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The goal of peer review is to…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Grade peers\",\r\n      \"Provide constructive feedback to improve writing\",\r\n      \"Eliminate grammar errors only\",\r\n      \"Replace teacher feedback\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Peers provide feedback, not grades.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Peer review helps writers improve through constructive, specific feedback from classmates.\",\r\n      \"While grammar matters, peer review addresses all aspects of writing.\",\r\n      \"Peer review complements, not replaces, teacher feedback.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q166\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Differentiated instruction means…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Teaching everyone the same way\",\r\n      \"Tailoring lessons to individual learning needs\",\r\n      \"Assigning extra credit\",\r\n      \"Reducing standards\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Differentiation involves varying instruction, not uniformity.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Differentiated instruction adjusts teaching to meet diverse student needs, interests, and readiness levels.\",\r\n      \"Extra credit is one strategy but doesn't define differentiation.\",\r\n      \"Differentiation maintains high standards while varying approaches.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q167\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Summative assessments are used to…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Adjust ongoing teaching\",\r\n      \"Provide mid-unit feedback\",\r\n      \"Evaluate overall achievement at the end of instruction\",\r\n      \"Record attendance\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Adjusting ongoing teaching is the purpose of formative assessment.\",\r\n      \"Mid-unit feedback is formative, not summative.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Summative assessments measure learning outcomes after instruction concludes.\",\r\n      \"Attendance tracking is administrative, not assessment.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q168\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Teaching and Assessment\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Reading conferences help teachers…\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Punish late readers\",\r\n      \"Discuss comprehension and growth individually\",\r\n      \"Avoid grading\",\r\n      \"Assign more work\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Conferences support learning, not punishment.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Reading conferences provide individualized assessment and goal-setting opportunities.\",\r\n      \"Conferences are a form of authentic assessment, not avoidance.\",\r\n      \"While teachers may assign work, the purpose is individualized support.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2,\r\n    \"assessment\": true\r\n  }\r\n  ,\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q301\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which device repeats initial consonant sounds?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Assonance\",\r\n      \"Alliteration\",\r\n      \"Consonance\",\r\n      \"Parallelism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Assonance repeats vowel sounds, not initial consonants.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Alliteration is the repetition of initial consonant sounds in neighboring words.\",\r\n      \"Consonance repeats consonant sounds at the end or middle of words.\",\r\n      \"Parallelism refers to similar grammatical structures, not sound repetition.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q302\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The phrase 'The rain in Spain falls mainly on the plain' illustrates:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Anaphora\",\r\n      \"Assonance\",\r\n      \"Metaphor\",\r\n      \"Juxtaposition\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Anaphora repeats words at the beginning of clauses, not vowel sounds.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Assonance is the repetition of vowel sounds ('ai' sound) within words.\",\r\n      \"This is not a metaphor; there's no comparison being made.\",\r\n      \"Juxtaposition places contrasting elements side by side, which isn't present here.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q303\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Repetition of consonant sounds at the ends of words is called:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Chiasmus\",\r\n      \"Consonance\",\r\n      \"Alliteration\",\r\n      \"Antithesis\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Chiasmus is inverted parallelism, not sound repetition.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Consonance repeats consonant sounds at the end or middle of words.\",\r\n      \"Alliteration repeats initial consonant sounds, not ending sounds.\",\r\n      \"Antithesis contrasts ideas, not sound patterns.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q304\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which rhetorical device repeats words at the beginning of successive clauses?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Epistrophe\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus\",\r\n      \"Anaphora\",\r\n      \"Antithesis\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Epistrophe repeats words at the end of clauses, not the beginning.\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus reverses grammatical structures, not simply repeats.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Anaphora repeats words at the beginning of successive clauses.\",\r\n      \"Antithesis contrasts ideas rather than repeating words.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q305\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Repetition at the end of successive clauses is called:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Anaphora\",\r\n      \"Epistrophe\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus\",\r\n      \"Parallelism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Anaphora repeats at the beginning, not the end.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Epistrophe repeats words at the end of successive clauses.\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus reverses structures rather than repeating at the end.\",\r\n      \"Parallelism uses similar structures but doesn't specifically repeat at the end.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q306\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which example demonstrates parallel structure?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"She likes reading, writing, and jogging.\",\r\n      \"She likes reading and to jog.\",\r\n      \"She likes to read and jogging.\",\r\n      \"She likes reading then jog.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! All three items use the same grammatical form (gerunds).\",\r\n      \"Mixes gerund (reading) with infinitive (to jog) - not parallel.\",\r\n      \"Mixes infinitive (to read) with gerund (jogging) - not parallel.\",\r\n      \"Inconsistent verb forms - not parallel structure.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q307\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'It was the best of times, it was the worst of times' illustrates:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Chiasmus\",\r\n      \"Antithesis\",\r\n      \"Juxtaposition\",\r\n      \"Litotes\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Chiasmus involves structural reversal, not present here.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Antithesis places contrasting ideas (best/worst) in parallel structures.\",\r\n      \"While contrasting, this specifically uses parallel structure, making it antithesis.\",\r\n      \"Litotes affirms through negation, which isn't shown here.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q308\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'Never let a Fool Kiss You or a Kiss Fool You' is an example of:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Chiasmus\",\r\n      \"Antithesis\",\r\n      \"Parallelism\",\r\n      \"Synecdoche\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Chiasmus reverses the word order (Fool Kiss / Kiss Fool).\",\r\n      \"Antithesis contrasts ideas but doesn't reverse structures.\",\r\n      \"While parallel, the key feature is the reversal, making it chiasmus.\",\r\n      \"Synecdoche uses parts for wholes, not relevant here.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q309\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which compares two unlike things directly?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Simile\",\r\n      \"Metaphor\",\r\n      \"Analogy\",\r\n      \"Euphemism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Similes use 'like' or 'as' for comparison, not direct.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Metaphors make direct comparisons without 'like' or 'as'.\",\r\n      \"Analogies explain relationships, not direct comparisons.\",\r\n      \"Euphemisms substitute mild terms, not comparisons.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q310\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'Her eyes sparkled like diamonds' is a:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Simile\",\r\n      \"Metaphor\",\r\n      \"Hyperbole\",\r\n      \"Euphemism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! This uses 'like' to compare, making it a simile.\",\r\n      \"Metaphors don't use 'like' or 'as'.\",\r\n      \"While vivid, it's not an exaggeration.\",\r\n      \"Euphemisms soften harsh terms, not relevant here.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q311\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'The wind whispered through the trees' uses:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Irony\",\r\n      \"Hyperbole\",\r\n      \"Personification\",\r\n      \"Symbolism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"No ironic contrast between expectation and reality.\",\r\n      \"This isn't an exaggeration.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Wind is given the human quality of whispering.\",\r\n      \"While potentially symbolic, the primary device is personification.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q312\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which device exaggerates for emphasis?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Hyperbole\",\r\n      \"Understatement\",\r\n      \"Litotes\",\r\n      \"Paradox\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Hyperbole deliberately exaggerates for effect.\",\r\n      \"Understatement minimizes, opposite of exaggeration.\",\r\n      \"Litotes affirms through negation, not exaggeration.\",\r\n      \"Paradox presents contradictions, not exaggeration.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q313\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Calling a disastrous event 'a small setback' is:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Paradox\",\r\n      \"Understatement\",\r\n      \"Euphemism\",\r\n      \"Metonymy\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Paradox involves contradictory truths, not minimization.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Understatement deliberately minimizes significance.\",\r\n      \"While mild, understatement is more specific than euphemism here.\",\r\n      \"Metonymy substitutes related terms, not relevant.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q314\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'Jumbo shrimp' is an example of:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Oxymoron\",\r\n      \"Paradox\",\r\n      \"Irony\",\r\n      \"Synecdoche\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Oxymoron combines contradictory terms (jumbo = large, shrimp = small).\",\r\n      \"Paradox reveals truth through contradiction, not just word pairing.\",\r\n      \"While ironic, the specific device is oxymoron.\",\r\n      \"Synecdoche uses parts for wholes, not relevant.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q315\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'The more you learn, the less you know' expresses:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Paradox\",\r\n      \"Irony\",\r\n      \"Hyperbole\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! This paradox seems contradictory but reveals truth about knowledge.\",\r\n      \"While containing irony, the primary device is paradox.\",\r\n      \"Not an exaggeration, but a philosophical truth.\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus reverses structure, not present here.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q316\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'Let's all give him a hand' uses a part for a whole:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Metonymy\",\r\n      \"Synecdoche\",\r\n      \"Euphemism\",\r\n      \"Personification\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Metonymy substitutes associated terms, not parts for wholes.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Synecdoche uses 'hand' (part) to represent applause (whole).\",\r\n      \"Euphemisms soften harsh terms, not relevant here.\",\r\n      \"Personification gives human qualities to non-humans.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q317\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'The White House issued a statement' replaces an institution for people. This is:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Synecdoche\",\r\n      \"Metonymy\",\r\n      \"Irony\",\r\n      \"Juxtaposition\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Synecdoche uses parts for wholes, not associations.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Metonymy substitutes 'White House' (place) for the President/administration.\",\r\n      \"No ironic contrast present.\",\r\n      \"Juxtaposition places contrasting elements together.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q318\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'That's not bad at all' is an example of:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Litotes\",\r\n      \"Euphemism\",\r\n      \"Understatement\",\r\n      \"Paradox\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Litotes affirms by denying the opposite ('not bad' = good).\",\r\n      \"Euphemisms soften harsh terms, not the same as negation.\",\r\n      \"While understated, the specific device using negation is litotes.\",\r\n      \"Paradox involves contradictions, not simple negation.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q319\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A rhetorical question is used to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Solicit a response\",\r\n      \"Confuse the audience\",\r\n      \"Emphasize a point\",\r\n      \"Transition to a new topic\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Rhetorical questions don't expect answers.\",\r\n      \"They clarify rather than confuse.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Rhetorical questions emphasize points without expecting answers.\",\r\n      \"While they can transition, emphasis is the primary purpose.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q320\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"'O Death, where is thy sting?' demonstrates:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Apostrophe\",\r\n      \"Metonymy\",\r\n      \"Antithesis\",\r\n      \"Symbolism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Apostrophe directly addresses Death as if it were present.\",\r\n      \"Metonymy substitutes related terms, not direct address.\",\r\n      \"Antithesis contrasts ideas, not present here.\",\r\n      \"While Death may be symbolic, the device is apostrophe.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q321\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Placing 'love and hate' side by side for emphasis is:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Juxtaposition\",\r\n      \"Parallelism\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus\",\r\n      \"Euphemism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Juxtaposition places contrasting elements together for emphasis.\",\r\n      \"Parallelism uses similar structures, but the emphasis here is on contrast.\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus reverses structure, not simply contrasting.\",\r\n      \"Euphemisms soften harsh terms, not relevant.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q322\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The contrast between what's expected and what occurs defines:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Irony\",\r\n      \"Paradox\",\r\n      \"Hyperbole\",\r\n      \"Symbolism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Irony is the contrast between expectation and reality.\",\r\n      \"Paradox reveals truth through seeming contradiction.\",\r\n      \"Hyperbole exaggerates, not about expectations.\",\r\n      \"Symbolism represents abstract ideas, not expectations.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q323\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"When an author repeats similar grammatical forms, they're using:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Parallelism\",\r\n      \"Anaphora\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus\",\r\n      \"Antithesis\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Parallelism uses similar grammatical structures.\",\r\n      \"Anaphora specifically repeats words at the beginning, not just structure.\",\r\n      \"Chiasmus reverses structure rather than repeating it.\",\r\n      \"Antithesis contrasts ideas, not just structure.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q324\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which device intentionally softens harsh realities?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Euphemism\",\r\n      \"Irony\",\r\n      \"Paradox\",\r\n      \"Understatement\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Euphemisms replace harsh terms with milder alternatives.\",\r\n      \"Irony contrasts expectations, doesn't soften.\",\r\n      \"Paradox presents contradictions, doesn't soften.\",\r\n      \"Understatement minimizes but doesn't specifically soften harsh terms.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q325\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Rhetorical Devices and Craft Techniques\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Diction contributes most directly to a text's:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Setting\",\r\n      \"Tone\",\r\n      \"Theme\",\r\n      \"Conflict\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Setting involves time and place, not word choice.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Diction (word choice) directly creates and shapes tone.\",\r\n      \"Theme is the central idea, influenced by many elements.\",\r\n      \"Conflict is the struggle, not directly determined by diction.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  }\r\n  ,\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q301\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Krashen's theory stating learners need input slightly above their level is called:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Input Hypothesis\",\r\n      \"Output Hypothesis\",\r\n      \"Monitor Model\",\r\n      \"Transfer Theory\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The Input Hypothesis (i+1) states learners acquire language when exposed to comprehensible input just beyond their current level.\",\r\n      \"Output Hypothesis emphasizes language production, not input.\",\r\n      \"Monitor Model is Krashen's broader framework, not specifically about input levels.\",\r\n      \"Transfer Theory addresses L1 influence, not input complexity.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q302\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The emotional factor that can block language learning is known as:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Critical Period\",\r\n      \"Affective Filter\",\r\n      \"Natural Order\",\r\n      \"Code-Switching\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Critical Period refers to optimal age for language acquisition.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The Affective Filter represents emotional barriers (anxiety, low motivation) that prevent input from being acquired.\",\r\n      \"Natural Order describes the sequence of grammar acquisition.\",\r\n      \"Code-Switching is alternating between languages, not an emotional barrier.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q303\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which strategy best supports students developing academic language (CALP)?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Playground talk\",\r\n      \"Scaffolded discussion using academic vocabulary\",\r\n      \"Total Physical Response\",\r\n      \"Peer imitation\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Playground talk develops BICS, not CALP.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Scaffolded academic discussions with explicit vocabulary instruction build CALP.\",\r\n      \"TPR is effective for comprehension but doesn't target academic language specifically.\",\r\n      \"Peer imitation may support social language but doesn't ensure academic language development.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q304\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A teacher gives clear examples of how to form past tense before student practice. This is:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Modeling\",\r\n      \"Drilling\",\r\n      \"Code-switching\",\r\n      \"Translation\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Modeling demonstrates correct language use before students attempt it independently.\",\r\n      \"Drilling involves repetitive practice, not initial demonstration.\",\r\n      \"Code-switching is language alternation, not instructional demonstration.\",\r\n      \"Translation converts meaning between languages, not demonstrating form.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q305\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Pairing language commands with movement is part of:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Silent Way\",\r\n      \"Direct Method\",\r\n      \"Total Physical Response\",\r\n      \"Communicative Approach\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Silent Way minimizes teacher talk, doesn't emphasize movement.\",\r\n      \"Direct Method uses target language only, but doesn't focus on physical response.\",\r\n      \"Correct! TPR uses physical actions to reinforce language meaning and comprehension.\",\r\n      \"Communicative Approach emphasizes interaction, not specifically movement.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q306\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Waiting several seconds before calling on ELL students improves:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Grammar\",\r\n      \"Processing time\",\r\n      \"Pronunciation\",\r\n      \"Fluency\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Wait time doesn't directly teach grammar.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Wait time gives ELLs processing time to think, translate internally, and formulate responses.\",\r\n      \"Pronunciation requires different interventions.\",\r\n      \"While helpful, the primary benefit is processing time, not immediate fluency.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q307\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Basic Interpersonal Communication Skills (BICS) differ from CALP because they:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Require academic vocabulary\",\r\n      \"Develop more slowly\",\r\n      \"Are used in social settings\",\r\n      \"Depend on grammar rules\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"BICS uses everyday vocabulary, not academic vocabulary.\",\r\n      \"BICS develops faster (1-2 years) than CALP (5-7 years).\",\r\n      \"Correct! BICS is conversational, context-embedded language used in social situations.\",\r\n      \"Both require grammar, but BICS is less formal and academic.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q308\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"When a Spanish-speaking student writes 'espectator' for 'spectator,' this reflects:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Language transfer\",\r\n      \"Code-switching\",\r\n      \"Fossilization\",\r\n      \"Overgeneralization\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Language transfer occurs when L1 patterns influence L2 production (Spanish 'espectador' → 'espectator').\",\r\n      \"Code-switching is intentional alternation between languages, not an error.\",\r\n      \"Fossilization is persistent incorrect forms despite instruction.\",\r\n      \"Overgeneralization applies learned rules too broadly, not L1 influence.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q309\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Teachers can support second language acquisition most effectively by:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Focusing only on grammar\",\r\n      \"Providing comprehensible input and context\",\r\n      \"Avoiding visuals\",\r\n      \"Limiting peer interaction\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Grammar focus alone doesn't ensure communicative competence.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Comprehensible input with rich context supports natural acquisition.\",\r\n      \"Visuals are essential supports for ELLs.\",\r\n      \"Peer interaction builds communicative competence.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q310\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Encouraging peer collaboration and discussion builds:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Morphology\",\r\n      \"Communicative competence\",\r\n      \"Syntax\",\r\n      \"Phonemic awareness\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Morphology is word structure, not built primarily through discussion.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Collaboration develops the ability to use language appropriately in social contexts.\",\r\n      \"Syntax benefits, but communicative competence is the broader outcome.\",\r\n      \"Phonemic awareness involves sound manipulation, not discussion.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q311\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An affix attached to the beginning of a word is called a:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Suffix\",\r\n      \"Prefix\",\r\n      \"Root\",\r\n      \"Stem\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Suffixes attach at the end.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Prefixes attach at the beginning (un-, re-, pre-).\",\r\n      \"Roots are the base meaning units.\",\r\n      \"Stems are bases to which affixes attach.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q312\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which term means an affix added to the end of a root?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Prefix\",\r\n      \"Suffix\",\r\n      \"Inflection\",\r\n      \"Stem\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Prefixes attach at the beginning.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Suffixes attach at the end (-ness, -tion, -ly).\",\r\n      \"Inflection is a type of suffix, but suffix is the general term.\",\r\n      \"Stems are not affixes.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q313\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which suffix changes a verb to a noun?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"-ize\",\r\n      \"-tion\",\r\n      \"-ful\",\r\n      \"-ous\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"-ize creates verbs from nouns/adjectives.\",\r\n      \"Correct! -tion creates nouns from verbs (act → action).\",\r\n      \"-ful creates adjectives from nouns.\",\r\n      \"-ous creates adjectives from nouns.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q314\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The suffix '-ize' typically forms:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Nouns\",\r\n      \"Verbs\",\r\n      \"Adjectives\",\r\n      \"Adverbs\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Nouns are formed by -tion, -ness, -ment.\",\r\n      \"Correct! -ize forms verbs (modern → modernize).\",\r\n      \"Adjectives use -ful, -ous, -able.\",\r\n      \"Adverbs use -ly.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q315\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The suffix '-ous' turns a noun into an:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Adjective\",\r\n      \"Verb\",\r\n      \"Adverb\",\r\n      \"Pronoun\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! -ous creates adjectives (danger → dangerous).\",\r\n      \"-ize, -en, -ify create verbs.\",\r\n      \"-ly typically creates adverbs.\",\r\n      \"Pronouns are not formed with suffixes.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q316\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which classroom activity best supports morphological awareness?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Memorizing spelling lists\",\r\n      \"Sorting words by affixes\",\r\n      \"Copying sentences\",\r\n      \"Reciting rhymes\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Memorization doesn't build awareness of word structure.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Sorting by affixes helps students analyze and understand word parts.\",\r\n      \"Copying doesn't emphasize morphological analysis.\",\r\n      \"Rhymes support phonological awareness, not morphological.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q317\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which morpheme type changes word meaning but not grammatical role?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Inflectional\",\r\n      \"Derivational\",\r\n      \"Compound\",\r\n      \"Bound root\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Inflectional morphemes indicate grammar (tense, number) without changing meaning.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Derivational morphemes create new words and meanings (happy → happiness).\",\r\n      \"Compounds combine two words, not morphemes.\",\r\n      \"Bound roots don't change meaning, they carry core meaning.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q318\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Adding '-ed' to indicate past tense is an example of a:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Derivational morpheme\",\r\n      \"Inflectional morpheme\",\r\n      \"Prefix\",\r\n      \"Suffix root\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Derivational morphemes change word class or meaning.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Inflectional morphemes mark grammar (tense, number, comparison) without changing word class.\",\r\n      \"Prefixes attach at the beginning.\",\r\n      \"This is an inflectional suffix, not a root.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q319\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Students comparing English and Spanish word endings practice:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Syntax\",\r\n      \"Metalinguistic awareness\",\r\n      \"Fluency\",\r\n      \"Phonology\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Syntax is sentence structure, not word analysis.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Metalinguistic awareness is consciously thinking about language structures.\",\r\n      \"Fluency is speed and accuracy, not language analysis.\",\r\n      \"Phonology is sound systems, not morphology.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q320\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A classroom focused on conversation, gestures, and visual aids demonstrates:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Grammar-Translation\",\r\n      \"Communicative approach\",\r\n      \"Audio-lingual method\",\r\n      \"Direct method\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Grammar-Translation emphasizes written translation and explicit grammar.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The Communicative approach emphasizes meaningful interaction and context.\",\r\n      \"Audio-lingual method focuses on drills and repetition.\",\r\n      \"Direct method uses target language only, without emphasizing visuals/gestures as much.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q321\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Teachers lower the affective filter by:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Increasing test frequency\",\r\n      \"Creating a supportive environment\",\r\n      \"Correcting every error publicly\",\r\n      \"Reducing peer interaction\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Frequent testing can raise anxiety.\",\r\n      \"Correct! A supportive, low-anxiety environment lowers the affective filter and promotes acquisition.\",\r\n      \"Public correction raises anxiety.\",\r\n      \"Peer interaction supports language development.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q322\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Providing sentence frames such as 'I think ___ because ___' helps students:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Build syntax and academic discourse\",\r\n      \"Practice phonics\",\r\n      \"Drill pronunciation\",\r\n      \"Avoid code-switching\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Sentence frames scaffold academic language structure and reasoning.\",\r\n      \"Phonics involves sound-letter relationships, not sentence structure.\",\r\n      \"Pronunciation is separate from sentence structure.\",\r\n      \"Sentence frames support language use, not eliminate code-switching.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q323\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Development, Second Language Acquisition, and Word Formation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"When teachers explicitly discuss word roots and affixes, they develop:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Morphological awareness\",\r\n      \"Phonemic fluency\",\r\n      \"Syntactic transfer\",\r\n      \"Listening comprehension\"\r\n    ],\r\n    