 \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which era of English followed the Norman Conquest and saw major French influence?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Old English\",\r\n      \"Middle English\",\r\n      \"Modern English\",\r\n      \"Contemporary English\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Old English (450-1100) predates the Norman Conquest.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Middle English (1100-1500) developed after the Norman Conquest brought French vocabulary into English.\",\r\n      \"Modern English came later, from 1500 onward.\",\r\n      \"Contemporary English is not a standard periodization term.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q327\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which event greatly affected vowel pronunciation between 1400–1600?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The Great Schism\",\r\n      \"Industrial Revolution\",\r\n      \"Great Vowel Shift\",\r\n      \"Norman Conquest\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The Great Schism was a religious division, not a linguistic change.\",\r\n      \"The Industrial Revolution occurred later (1760-1840) and affected society, not pronunciation.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The Great Vowel Shift changed how long vowels were pronounced, creating spelling-pronunciation mismatches.\",\r\n      \"The Norman Conquest (1066) affected vocabulary but not this specific vowel change.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q328\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The Renaissance and Shakespeare contributed to the rise of:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Old English\",\r\n      \"Early Modern English\",\r\n      \"Middle English\",\r\n      \"Contemporary English\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Old English ended around 1100 CE.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Early Modern English (1500-1800) includes the Renaissance, Shakespeare, and early standardization.\",\r\n      \"Middle English ended around 1500 CE.\",\r\n      \"Contemporary English came later.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q329\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A word adopted directly from another language, such as 'ballet', is called a:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Calque\",\r\n      \"Loanword\",\r\n      \"Creole\",\r\n      \"Derivation\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Calques translate structure, not adopt words directly.\",\r\n      \"Correct! A loanword is borrowed unchanged from another language (ballet, piano, café).\",\r\n      \"Creole is a type of language, not a borrowed word.\",\r\n      \"Derivation creates new words from existing roots, not borrowing.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q330\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A word-for-word translation of a foreign phrase is known as a:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Pidgin\",\r\n      \"Calque\",\r\n      \"Borrowing\",\r\n      \"Neologism\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Pidgin is a simplified contact language, not a translation method.\",\r\n      \"Correct! A calque translates the structure literally (skyscraper = gratte-ciel).\",\r\n      \"Borrowing is general; calque is specifically 