tIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Dialects are regional/social varieties, not evolved pidgins.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Creoles develop when pidgins gain native speakers and expand grammatically.\",\r\n      \"Idiolects are individual speech patterns.\",\r\n      \"Sociolects are social group varieties.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q334\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following best defines 'dialect'?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"A social language register\",\r\n      \"A simplified communication form\",\r\n      \"A regional or social language variety\",\r\n      \"A new language created from two others\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Register refers to formality level, not dialect.\",\r\n      \"This describes pidgin, not dialect.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Dialects are systematic regional or social varieties of a language.\",\r\n      \"This describes a creole or mixed language.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q335\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An individual's unique way of speaking is referred to as their:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Sociolect\",\r\n      \"Dialect\",\r\n      \"Idiolect\",\r\n      \"Register\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Sociolect is group-based, not individual.\",\r\n      \"Dialect is regional/social, not individual.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Idiolect is an individual's unique linguistic fingerprint.\",\r\n      \"Register is formality level, not individual style.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q336\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Language associated with specific social groups or classes is called a:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Pidgin\",\r\n      \"Sociolect\",\r\n      \"Dialect\",\r\n      \"Register\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Pidgin is a contact language, not a social variety.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Sociolect reflects social class, education, or group membership.\",\r\n      \"Dialect is regional, not specifically social.\",\r\n      \"Register is formality level.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q337\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which statement best defines slang?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"A fixed formal register\",\r\n      \"A type of professional jargon\",\r\n      \"Informal language used for identity or novelty\",\r\n      \"Words borrowed from Latin\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Slang is informal, not formal.\",\r\n      \"Jargon is specialized; slang is broader and more informal.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Slang is informal, group-specific, and evolves rapidly.\",\r\n      \"Latin borrowings are not necessarily slang.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q338\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The phrase 'cool' meaning 'good' or 'stylish' is an example of:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"A regionalism\",\r\n      \"A neologism\",\r\n      \"Slang\",\r\n      \"Borrowing\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Not limited to one region.\",\r\n      \"It's not newly coined, but repurposed.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'Cool' for 'good' is informal slang that emerged mid-20th century.\",\r\n      \"It's not borrowed from another language.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q339\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The word 'rodeo' entered English from:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"German\",\r\n      \"Spanish\",\r\n      \"French\",\r\n      \"Italian\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"German did not contribute 'rodeo.'\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'Rodeo' comes from Spanish, reflecting cultural exchange in the American West.\",\r\n      \"Not from French.\",\r\n      \"Not from Italian.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q340\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Development of English Language, Borrowings, and Regional Variation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Variation between 'pop' and 'soda' across U.S. regions demonstrates:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Morphology\",\r\n      \"Lexical variation\",\r\n      \"Phonemic drift\",\r\n      \"Idiolect\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Morphology is word structure, not regional word choice.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Lexical variation means different words for the same thing in different regions.\",\r\n      \"Phonemic drift is sound change over time.\",\r\n      \"Idiolect is individual, not regional.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  }\r\n  ,\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q201\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Acquisition, Transfer, and Cross-Curricular Language Development\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which theory emphasizes imitation and reinforcement in language learning?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\"Behaviorist\", \"Nativist\", \"Interactionist\", 