isition.\",\r\n      \"Sociocultural emphasizes cultural context, not biological structures.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q204\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Acquisition, Transfer, and Cross-Curricular Language Development\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Language learning occurring through social interaction reflects:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\"Interactionist Theory\", \"Behaviorist Theory\", \"Cognitive Theory\", \"Innate Hypothesis\"],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Interactionist theory emphasizes that language develops through meaningful social interaction.\",\r\n      \"Behaviorist focuses on imitation/reinforcement, not social meaning-making.\",\r\n      \"Cognitive theory links language to mental development, not specifically social interaction.\",\r\n      \"Innate Hypothesis (nativist) emphasizes biological capacity, not interaction.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q205\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Acquisition, Transfer, and Cross-Curricular Language Development\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The belief that early childhood is the most effective period for language learning supports the:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\"Critical Period Hypothesis\", \"Monitor Model\", \"Input Hypothesis\", \"Communicative Approach\"],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The Critical Period Hypothesis states language acquisition is easiest during childhood before brain plasticity declines.\",\r\n      \"Monitor Model addresses conscious language learning, not age factors.\",\r\n      \"Input Hypothesis focuses on comprehensible input, not developmental timing.\",\r\n      \"Communicative Approach is a teaching method, not a developmental theory.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q206\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Acquisition, Transfer, and Cross-Curricular Language Development\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An English learner using Spanish grammar patterns in English speech demonstrates:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\"Positive transfer\", \"Negative transfer\", \"Fossilization\", \"Code-mixing\"],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Positive transfer helps learning through similarities, not creates errors.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Negative transfer (interference) occurs when L1 patterns cause errors in L2.\",\r\n      \"Fossilization is persistent errors despite instruction, not initial transfer.\",\r\n      \"Code-mixing blends languages intentionally, not creates grammar errors.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q207\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Acquisition, Transfer, and Cross-Curricular Language Development\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"When similarities between two languages aid learning, this is:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\"Positive transfer\", \"Negative transfer\", \"Interlanguage\", \"Code-switching\"],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Positive transfer occurs when L1-L2 similarities facilitate learning (e.g., cognates).\",\r\n      \"Negative transfer causes difficulties, not aids learning.\",\r\n      \"Interlanguage is the learner's evolving system, not the transfer itself.\",\r\n      \"Code-switching alternates languages, not transfer of patterns.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q208\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Acquisition, Transfer, and Cross-Curricular Language Development\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The transitional system combining elements of L1 and L2 is called:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\"Code-switching\", \"Interlanguage\", \"Pidgin\", \"Register\"],\r\n    \"co