skills while reinforcing authentic audience awareness.\",\r\n      \"Historical literary analysis may occur but is not the main benefit of the blog activity.\",\r\n      \"Memorization is not a focus; the activity emphasizes interaction and communication.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q632\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understand the integrated nature of the English language arts\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An English teacher asks students to create short podcasts explaining the themes of assigned novels and to include brief quotations to support their points. This approach best fosters:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"oral fluency through repeated reading practice.\",\r\n      \"integrated comprehension by synthesizing speaking, reading, and writing skills.\",\r\n      \"technical editing of recorded audio files.\",\r\n      \"memorization of literary quotations for assessment purposes.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Oral fluency may improve, but the main goal is integrating multiple literacy skills.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Students read to gather evidence, write to plan their script, and speak to communicate analysis—pure language arts integration.\",\r\n      \"Technical editing is a side skill, not the primary educational focus.\",\r\n      \"Memorization is not the goal; synthesis and communication of ideas are the focus.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q633\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"During the Old English period, the language was most heavily influenced by:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Latin brought by Roman missionaries\",\r\n      \"Norse vocabulary introduced through Viking contact\",\r\n      \"Celtic grammatical structures\",\r\n      \"Norman French syntax\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While Latin influenced Old English through Christianity, it was not the most pervasive influence.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The Viking invasions infused Old English with Norse loanwords like 'sky,' 'egg,' and 'window,' shaping northern dialects.\",\r\n      \"Celtic had minimal impact on Old English grammar; most Celtic influence was place names.\",\r\n      \"Norman French came later during the Middle English period, not Old English.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q634\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which event most contributed to the standardization of English spelling and grammar?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The Great Vowel Shift\",\r\n      \"The introduction of the printing press by William Caxton in 1476\",\r\n      \"The Protestant Reformation\",\r\n      \"The publication of Johnson's Dictionary of the English Language in 1755\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The Great Vowel Shift changed pronunciation but did not standardize spelling.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The printing press made written English more uniform and spread London's dialect as a standard.\",\r\n      \"The Reformation influenced Bible translation but was not the primary standardization force.\",\r\n      \"Johnson's Dictionary was important but came after printing had already established many standards.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q635\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which development most clearly reflects the influence of global expansion on the English language?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The adoption of words like café and rendezvous\",\r\n      \"The inclusion of loanwords from Hindi, Arabic, and Swahili\",\r\n      \"The simplification of English verb conjugations\",\r\n      \"The creation of Middle English poetic forms\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"French borrowings reflect European contact, not global expansion.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Colonial contact introduced vocabulary such as 'bungalow,' 'algebra,' and 'safari,' demonstrating English's global borrowing.\",\r\n      \"Verb simplification was an internal development, not due to global expansion.\",\r\n      \"Middle English poetry was a literary development, not related to global contact.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q636\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which factor most contributed to the divergence of British and American English spelling conventions?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The establishment of colonial universities\",\r\n      \"Noah Webster's dictionary reforms\",\r\n      \"The Industrial Revolution\",\r\n      \"Early missionary translation projects\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Colonial universities preserved British conventions rather than diverging from them.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Webster intentionally simplified spellings—'color' vs. 'colour'—to promote linguistic independence in the new nation.\",\r\n      \"The Industrial Revolution affected vocabulary but not systematic spelling differences.\",\r\n      \"Missionary projects did not focus on spelling reform.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q637\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following best explains the rapid expansion of new vocabulary during the 20th century?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Growth of literacy and public schooling\",\r\n      \"Technological and scientific innovation\",\r\n      \"Simplification of English grammar\",\r\n      \"Increased translation of classical texts\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Literacy growth helped spread language but did not create new vocabulary.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Terms like 'television,' 'internet,' and 'genetic code' arose with modern inventions and discoveries.\",\r\n      \"Grammar simplification is unrelated to vocabulary expansion.\",\r\n      \"Classical translations added some terms but not at the pace of modern technology.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q638\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which statement best illustrates how social movements shape language?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Increased borrowing from Latin during the Renaissance\",\r\n      \"The emergence of gender-inclusive pronouns such as they for singular use\",\r\n      \"The creation of dictionaries to define 'standard' language\",\r\n      \"The replacement of Middle English endings with Modern English ones\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Latin borrowing was driven by scholarly interests, not social movements.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Contemporary social awareness influences evolving norms of identity and expression.\",\r\n      \"Dictionary creation reflected prescriptive authority, not social movements.\",\r\n      \"Grammar changes from Middle to Modern English were evolutionary, not movement-driven.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q639\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following is an example of English as a living, evolving language?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The preservation of Old English in historical archives\",\r\n      \"The continued use of Shakespearean English in modern writing\",\r\n      \"The emergence of internet slang and new verb forms such as 'to google'\",\r\n      \"The adoption of prescriptive grammar handbooks in schools\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Preservation of Old English maintains history but does not show evolution.\",\r\n      \"Using Shakespearean English is archaic practice, not evolution.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Digital communication continuously produces new vocabulary and usage patterns.\",\r\n      \"Prescriptive handbooks attempt to freeze language, not demonstrate evolution.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q640\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which statement best reflects how regional dialects develop within a single language?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"They arise from deliberate educational reforms.\",\r\n      \"They result from isolation and distinct cultural contact patterns.\",\r\n      \"They emerge only in rural communities.\",\r\n      \"They reflect the random variation of individual speakers.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Educational reforms try to standardize, not create dialects.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Geography, migration, and community interaction create region-specific pronunciations and idioms.\",\r\n      \"Dialects develop in both urban and rural areas.\",\r\n      \"Dialect formation is systematic, not random individual variation.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q641\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The English used by writers of the Harlem Renaissance (e.g., Langston Hughes, Zora Neale Hurston) demonstrates:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"rejection of African linguistic heritage\",\r\n      \"codification of formal standard English\",\r\n      \"creative adaptation of vernacular speech to convey cultural identity\",\r\n      \"resistance to all linguistic experimentation\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Harlem Renaissance writers embraced African linguistic heritage.\",\r\n      \"They challenged formal conventions rather than codifying them.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Writers blended African American vernacular with literary English to express cultural voice and authenticity.\",\r\n      \"Harlem Renaissance was marked by linguistic experimentation, not resistance to it.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q642\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"History and development of the English language\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"In contemporary linguistics, the term World Englishes refers to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Attempts to restore classical grammar in global education\",\r\n      \"Localized varieties of English shaped by regional and cultural factors\",\r\n      \"The replacement of native languages with English worldwide\",\r\n      \"Simplified English used exclusively in diplomacy\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"World Englishes recognizes diversity, not restoration of classical grammar.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'World Englishes' recognizes that British, Indian, Nigerian, and Singaporean English are all legitimate, evolving varieties.\",\r\n      \"The term celebrates multilingualism, not language replacement.\",\r\n      \"World Englishes includes all varieties, not just simplified diplomatic English.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q643\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A teacher organizes a literature unit including The Joy Luck Club by Amy Tan, Bless Me, Ultima by Rudolfo Anaya, and The Namesake by Jhumpa Lahiri. The teacher's primary purpose in selecting these works is to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"expose students to novels written exclusively in first person.\",\r\n      \"highlight the influence of myth and folklore in contemporary fiction.\",\r\n      \"explore the experiences of cultural identity and assimilation in the United States.\",\r\n      \"teach students how to apply postmodern literary theory.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While some may use first person, narrative perspective is not the unifying theme.\",\r\n      \"Myth and folklore appear but are not the primary focus across all three novels.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Each novel portrays generational and cultural negotiation—ideal for discussing identity and assimilation.\",\r\n      \"Postmodern theory is not the main instructional goal of this selection.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q644\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A high-school class reads The Diary of Anne Frank alongside Night by Elie Wiesel. The teacher's goal is most likely to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"demonstrate different approaches to narrative structure.\",\r\n      \"examine personal and historical perspectives on human rights and moral courage.\",\r\n      \"introduce students to post-World War II literary techniques.\",\r\n      \"analyze translation differences between European and American editions.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While structure varies, it is not the primary educational purpose of pairing these texts.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Pairing survivor accounts helps students connect literature to ethical and historical understanding.\",\r\n      \"Literary techniques are secondary to the human rights and moral themes.\",\r\n      \"Translation analysis is not the focus of this pairing.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q645\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An English teacher assigns Their Eyes Were Watching God and asks students to compare it with modern spoken-word poetry exploring similar themes. This assignment primarily helps students:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"practice formal citation in research essays.\",\r\n      \"identify social commentary on gender and voice across time periods.\",\r\n      \"memorize dialect for oral performance.\",\r\n      \"evaluate publishing trends in African American literature.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Citation practice is a skill outcome, not the primary purpose of thematic comparison.\",\r\n      \"Correct! It links historical and contemporary expressions of autonomy and voice—illustrating literature's social purpose.\",\r\n      \"Memorization of dialect is not the instructional goal.\",\r\n      \"Publishing trends are not the focus of this thematic comparison.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q646\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A teacher selects several environmental poems and essays, such as those by Mary Oliver and Wendell Berry, for a thematic unit titled 'Literature of Place.' The main instructional purpose of this unit is to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"demonstrate scientific accuracy in literary description.\",\r\n      \"connect students' reading to ecological awareness and personal reflection.\",\r\n      \"teach metrical variation in free verse poetry.\",\r\n      \"focus on the economic factors influencing rural literature.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Scientific accuracy is not the primary goal of literary study.\",\r\n      \"Correct! It integrates literature with environmental ethics and reflective thinking.\",\r\n      \"Meter is a technical element, not the main purpose of this thematic unit.\",\r\n      \"Economic factors are not the central focus of environmental literature study.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q647\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the purposes and values of teaching literature\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"During a world-literature course, students read Things Fall Apart by Chinua Achebe and discuss colonial perspectives portrayed in early British novels. The teacher's intent in this comparison is to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"reinforce grammar and vocabulary from non-Western sources.\",\r\n      \"expose students to non-Western viewpoints and post-colonial themes.\",\r\n      \"illustrate the chronological development of the English novel.\",\r\n      \"teach students translation methods for African languages.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Grammar and vocabulary are not the primary focus of this comparison.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Achebe's work reclaims narrative authority, showing literature's power to challenge dominant cultural perspectives.\",\r\n      \"Chronological development is secondary to examining cultural perspectives.\",\r\n      \"Translation methods are not the goal of this comparative study.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q648\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"In Wuthering Heights, Emily Brontë's use of multiple narrators and a non-linear structure primarily serves to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"create a sense of historical distance between reader and character.\",\r\n      \"emphasize the power of social hierarchy in determining fate.\",\r\n      \"present differing psychological perspectives that complicate moral judgment.\",\r\n      \"highlight the influence of classical myth on Romantic storytelling.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While historical distance exists, the primary purpose is psychological complexity, not temporal separation.\",\r\n      \"Social hierarchy is a theme, but the narrative structure's main function is to present multiple viewpoints.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Brontë's framing devices (Lockwood and Nelly) invite readers to weigh conflicting viewpoints, deepening the novel's moral ambiguity.\",\r\n      \"Classical myth is not the primary influence on Brontë's narrative technique.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q649\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Elizabeth Gaskell's North and South explores industrialization and class conflict in Victorian England. Her portrayal of these social tensions is most significant because it:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"romanticizes the lives of working-class characters to promote idealism.\",\r\n      \"integrates social realism with moral sympathy to critique economic inequality.\",\r\n      \"rejects political engagement in favor of domestic harmony.\",\r\n      \"portrays the decline of rural England through comic irony.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Gaskell presents realistic, not romanticized, portrayals of working-class life.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Gaskell's realist approach humanizes labor issues and bridges moral and social commentary—an early example of industrial fiction.\",\r\n      \"The novel actively engages with political and economic issues rather than avoiding them.\",\r\n      \"Comic irony is not the dominant tone; Gaskell employs earnest social realism.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q650\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre was groundbreaking among nineteenth-century novels because it:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"portrayed a heroine who rejects both love and independence.\",\r\n      \"depicted a female protagonist whose moral and emotional autonomy challenge Victorian gender norms.\",\r\n      \"idealized romantic dependence as a form of social stability.\",\r\n      \"dismissed religious and moral struggle as irrelevant to personal growth.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Jane seeks both love and independence on her own terms, not rejecting either.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Jane's insistence on self-respect and conscience makes the novel an early feminist and psychological narrative.\",\r\n      \"The novel critiques, rather than idealizes, romantic dependence that compromises autonomy.\",\r\n      \"Religious and moral struggle are central to Jane's character development throughout the novel.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q651\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Emily Brontë's depiction of nature in Wuthering Heights differs from typical Romantic literature because it:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"presents the natural world as indifferent or violent rather than purely inspiring.\",\r\n      \"replaces imagery of rural beauty with industrial landscapes.\",\r\n      \"idealizes pastoral life as an escape from moral decay.\",\r\n      \"omits natural imagery to focus entirely on domestic interiors.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Her wild moorland setting mirrors the intensity of human passion and cruelty—nature as emotional landscape, not idyllic refuge.\",\r\n      \"The novel is set on the moors, not in industrial landscapes.\",\r\n      \"Brontë subverts pastoral idealization by presenting nature as harsh and unforgiving.\",\r\n      \"Natural imagery is central to the novel's atmosphere and symbolism.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q652\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"World Literature in English, exclusive of North America\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"In Mary Barton, Elizabeth Gaskell combines sentimental narrative techniques with journalistic observation to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"disguise political content beneath conventional romance.\",\r\n      \"argue that industrial progress inevitably leads to social harmony.\",\r\n      \"appeal to middle-class readers' empathy for working-class suffering.\",\r\n      \"portray factory life as an entertaining backdrop for personal ambition.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Rather than disguising political content, Gaskell makes it central and explicit through empathetic storytelling.\",\r\n      \"The novel critiques, not celebrates, industrial progress without social reform.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Gaskell uses empathy and realism to awaken social conscience among her readership—hallmarks of early social-problem fiction.\",\r\n      \"Factory life is portrayed seriously as a site of suffering, not entertainment.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q653\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"English syntax and sentence structure\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following sentences violates the conventions of English syntax?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The candle flickered softly in the wind.\",\r\n      \"Down the stairs fell the book with a thud.\",\r\n      \"They the park quickly across ran.\",\r\n      \"The dog barked loudly at the mail carrier.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This follows standard subject–verb–object order with proper syntax.\",\r\n      \"This uses inverted word order for emphasis, which is grammatically acceptable in English.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'They the park quickly across ran' scrambles normal English word order (subject–verb–object) and violates syntax conventions.\",\r\n      \"This follows standard English syntax with subject, verb, and adverbial phrase.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q654\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Parallel structure and sentence coherence\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which sentence demonstrates faulty parallel structure?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The hikers were cold, tired, and hungry for dinner.\",\r\n      \"She enjoys dancing, to swim, and playing tennis.\",\r\n      \"He came, he saw, he conquered.\",\r\n      \"The speech was long but inspiring.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This maintains parallel structure with three adjectives in a series.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Parallel structure requires consistent grammatical forms — this should be 'dancing, swimming, and playing' (all gerunds) or 'to dance, to swim, and to play' (all infinitives).\",\r\n      \"This demonstrates excellent parallel structure with three parallel clauses.\",\r\n      \"This uses parallel adjectives to describe the speech.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q655\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Sentence fragments and complete sentences\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following sentences contains a sentence fragment?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"After the rain stopped, the children ran outside.\",\r\n      \"Because she was late to the meeting.\",\r\n      \"The bus left at noon sharp.\",\r\n      \"The teacher collected the homework on time.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This is a complete sentence with a dependent clause followed by an independent clause.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'Because she was late to the meeting' is a dependent clause missing its main clause. It needs an independent clause to be complete.\",\r\n      \"This is a complete sentence with subject, verb, and additional details.\",\r\n      \"This is a complete sentence with subject, verb, and object.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q656\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Modifiers and clarity\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following sentences demonstrates a misplaced modifier?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"She nearly drove her car for ten hours without stopping.\",\r\n      \"The teacher handed the papers to the students on the desk.\",\r\n      \"Running quickly, the child caught the ball.\",\r\n      \"After lunch, the meeting resumed promptly.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While 'nearly' could be clearer if moved, the sentence meaning is relatively clear.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'On the desk' incorrectly modifies 'students,' implying they were sitting on the desk rather than the papers being on the desk. The modifier is misplaced.\",\r\n      \"The modifier 'Running quickly' correctly modifies 'the child.'\",\r\n      \"The modifier 'After lunch' correctly modifies when the meeting resumed.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q657\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Structural ambiguity and coordination\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following sentences is structurally ambiguous because of faulty coordination?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The coach praised the players, and the crowd cheered loudly.\",\r\n      \"She told him she loved her cat and so did he.\",\r\n      \"The storm passed quickly and left the sky clear.\",\r\n      \"He wanted to visit London and Paris during his trip.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This has clear coordination between two independent clauses with no ambiguity.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'She told him she loved her cat and so did he' could mean he also told her, or that he also loved her cat — structural ambiguity through coordination.\",\r\n      \"This clearly coordinates two actions performed by the storm.\",\r\n      \"This clearly coordinates two cities he wanted to visit.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q658\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Morphology and word structure\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following words contains two bound morphemes?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"unbelievable\",\r\n      \"happiness\",\r\n      \"walking\",\r\n      \"sunlight\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! 'Un-' and '-able' are both bound morphemes attached to the free base 'believe.' Neither can function as independent words.\",\r\n      \"This contains one bound morpheme ('-ness') attached to the free morpheme 'happy.'\",\r\n      \"This contains one bound morpheme ('-ing') attached to the free morpheme 'walk.'\",\r\n      \"This is a compound word containing two free morphemes ('sun' + 'light'), not bound morphemes.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q659\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Inflectional vs. derivational morphology\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following best illustrates inflectional morphology?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Change → changer\",\r\n      \"Teach → teacher\",\r\n      \"Quick → quickly\",\r\n      \"Walk → walked\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This is derivational morphology; '-er' creates a new noun from a verb.\",\r\n      \"This is derivational morphology; '-er' creates a new noun (person who teaches) from a verb.\",\r\n      \"This is derivational morphology; '-ly' changes an adjective into an adverb.\",\r\n      \"Correct! '-ed' marks past tense, an inflection that changes grammatical function but not lexical meaning. 'Walked' remains a verb with the same core meaning.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q660\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Syntactic ambiguity\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of these sentences demonstrates syntactic ambiguity created by phrase structure?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Visiting relatives can be exhausting.\",\r\n      \"The teacher collected the essays before lunch.\",\r\n      \"They ran down the hill quickly.\",\r\n      \"The book on the desk belongs to Maria.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! 'Visiting relatives' can function as a noun phrase (the act of visiting) or a participial phrase (relatives who are visiting). The phrase structure permits both interpretations.\",\r\n      \"This has clear phrase structure with no ambiguity about who collected what and when.\",\r\n      \"This has straightforward structure indicating manner and direction of running.\",\r\n      \"The prepositional phrase 'on the desk' clearly modifies 'book' with no ambiguity.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q661\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Derivational morphology\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following illustrates derivational morphology?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Run → running\",\r\n      \"Happy → unhappy\",\r\n      \"Cat → cats\",\r\n      \"Sleep → slept\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This is inflectional morphology; '-ing' creates the present participle form without changing core meaning.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Adding 'un-' creates a new word with a changed meaning (opposite), not just a grammatical variation. This is derivational morphology.\",\r\n      \"This is inflectional morphology; '-s' marks plural without changing the core meaning.\",\r\n      \"This is inflectional morphology; the vowel change marks past tense without creating a new word.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q662\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Semantic agreement\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which sentence violates semantic agreement (the logical relationship between subject and predicate)?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The idea was fascinating.\",\r\n      \"The committee have made its decision.\",\r\n      \"The thunder laughed across the sky.\",\r\n      \"The flowers were blooming early.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This has proper semantic agreement; ideas can be fascinating.\",\r\n      \"This has a subject-verb agreement error (number mismatch), not a semantic violation.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Thunder doesn't literally laugh — this is a semantic mismatch (though acceptable as figurative language/personification). The predicate is incompatible with the nature of the subject.\",\r\n      \"This has proper semantic agreement; flowers can bloom.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q663\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the active and constructive nature of viewing and visually representing information\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Students are comparing two television advertisements for the same product—one presented as a celebrity endorsement and the other as a short animated story. To help students understand how visual choices influence perception, which question would be most effective to ask first?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Which advertisement do you personally prefer?\",\r\n      \"What is each advertisement's main visual technique for persuasion?\",\r\n      \"How long is each advertisement?\",\r\n      \"How much did the company pay for celebrity participation?\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Personal preference doesn't focus on analyzing visual construction techniques.\",\r\n      \"Correct! This question directs attention to how visual construction conveys meaning—central to media analysis.\",\r\n      \"Duration is a technical detail that doesn't address how visuals shape perception.\",\r\n      \"Production costs are irrelevant to analyzing persuasive visual techniques.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q664\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the active and constructive nature of viewing and visually representing information\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"After watching a documentary about pollution, a teacher asks students to identify how the filmmaker's camera angles, lighting, and editing influenced their emotional response. This question mainly promotes students' ability to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"summarize factual information accurately.\",\r\n      \"identify visual techniques that shape viewer interpretation.\",\r\n      \"critique the accuracy of the documentary's data.\",\r\n      \"predict what might happen if pollution continues.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Summarizing facts doesn't address how visual techniques construct meaning and emotion.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Students learn that visual media doesn't just show—it constructs emotion and perspective through deliberate choices.\",\r\n      \"Data accuracy is separate from understanding how visual techniques influence viewer response.\",\r\n      \"Making predictions about pollution is unrelated to analyzing filmmaking techniques.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q665\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the active and constructive nature of viewing and visually representing information\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Students view two news clips about the same international event, one from a U.S. network and the other from a foreign broadcaster. The teacher asks, 'What differences do you notice in the way each network represents the people involved?' This question primarily helps students:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"practice distinguishing between facts and opinions.\",\r\n      \"evaluate the influence of cultural perspective on visual representation.\",\r\n      \"identify technical limitations of video production.\",\r\n      \"memorize global geography related to the event.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While related, the focus is specifically on representation and bias, not general fact-opinion distinction.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The focus is on representation and bias—a core part of visual literacy. Students learn how cultural perspective shapes what is shown and how it's portrayed.\",\r\n      \"Technical limitations don't address how different networks construct meaning through representation.\",\r\n      \"Geography memorization is unrelated to analyzing visual representation and bias.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q666\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the active and constructive nature of viewing and visually representing information\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A teacher asks students to design an infographic summarizing a short article on renewable energy. This activity most directly develops students' understanding of:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"how color, proportion, and layout affect communication.\",\r\n      \"how to cite digital sources in academic writing.\",\r\n      \"how to calculate data trends accurately.\",\r\n      \"how to critique authors' tone and mood.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Constructing visuals teaches how design choices shape message clarity and emphasis. Students actively experience how visual representation constructs meaning.\",\r\n      \"Citation skills are separate from understanding visual design principles.\",\r\n      \"Data calculation is a math skill, not visual representation analysis.\",\r\n      \"Critiquing tone relates to reading comprehension, not visual design principles.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q667\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Understanding the active and constructive nature of viewing and visually representing information\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Students view a short film with rapid editing and upbeat music. The teacher then asks, 'What mood or message do these visual and sound choices create?' This question best fosters students' ability to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"analyze how multimodal techniques influence audience response.\",\r\n      \"determine the literal sequence of events in the film.\",\r\n      \"memorize key dialogue for later discussion.\",\r\n      \"evaluate the technical quality of film editing.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Integrating sound and imagery analysis reinforces how meaning is actively built through visual composition and audio cues. This is multimodal literacy.\",\r\n      \"Determining event sequence focuses on comprehension, not how techniques shape response.\",\r\n      \"Memorizing dialogue doesn't address how visual and audio choices construct meaning.\",\r\n      \"Technical quality evaluation doesn't focus on how these choices influence audience interpretation.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q668\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language evolution and lexical development\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following is the most likely reason that the term streaming has become part of the Standard American English lexicon?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"It expresses a newly developed technology and everyday activity.\",\r\n      \"It originated as a slang term used only in music journalism.\",\r\n      \"It replaced a classical Latin term that was difficult to spell.\",\r\n      \"It has remained unchanged in meaning since the nineteenth century.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! 'Streaming' filled a new communicative need tied to digital media—exactly how English expands to accommodate technological and cultural changes.\",\r\n      \"While it may have appeared in specialized contexts first, it became mainstream because it describes a universal activity, not just journalism.\",\r\n      \"There was no difficult Latin term it replaced; it arose organically from the technological need.\",\r\n      \"The digital media sense of 'streaming' is recent, not from the nineteenth century.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q669\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language evolution and lexical development\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following newly coined words is most likely to become a permanent part of standard English?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"doomscrolling — obsessive reading of negative online news\",\r\n      \"fleeky-boo — a short-lived social-media catchphrase\",\r\n      \"quizzify — an invented term with no real application\",\r\n      \"scribblr — a single brand name with no generic meaning\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! It describes a recognizable human behavior in the digital era; utility and widespread recognition predict longevity in the lexicon.\",\r\n      \"Short-lived catchphrases typically fade without sustained use or clear utility.\",\r\n      \"Invented terms without practical application rarely gain traction in the language.\",\r\n      \"Brand names remain specific unless they become genericized (like 'xerox' or 'kleenex'), which requires widespread adoption.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q670\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language evolution and lexical development\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The widespread acceptance of terms such as blog, podcast, and selfie best illustrates which characteristic of the English language?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Its ability to generate new words to describe cultural innovations\",\r\n      \"Its reliance on foreign borrowings for vocabulary expansion\",\r\n      \"Its decline in grammatical precision over time\",\r\n      \"Its preference for formal, academic registers\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! English grows through creative adaptation to new technologies and experiences. These terms arose from technological and cultural shifts.\",\r\n      \"These are newly coined English words, not borrowings from other languages.\",\r\n      \"These new words don't indicate grammatical decline—they show lexical innovation and adaptability.\",\r\n      \"These terms emerged from informal, popular culture, not formal academic contexts.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q671\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Semantic change and language evolution\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following examples best demonstrates the process of semantic shift in English?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The word tweet changing from a bird sound to an online post\",\r\n      \"The word ballet entering English from French\",\r\n      \"The word run forming the noun runner\",\r\n      \"The word color spelled without the British u\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! 'Tweet' gained a new, technology-driven meaning—semantic change in action. The original meaning remains, but a new sense was added.\",\r\n      \"This is borrowing, not semantic shift. The word maintains its original meaning from French.\",\r\n      \"This is derivation (morphological word formation), not semantic shift.\",\r\n      \"This is spelling variation between dialects, not semantic change.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q672\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language evolution and social change\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The increasing use of gender-neutral pronouns such as they in singular contexts primarily reflects:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"a shift in grammatical rules caused by foreign influence.\",\r\n      \"resistance to prescriptive grammar and changing social norms.\",\r\n      \"a decline in written literacy skills.\",\r\n      \"confusion between spoken and written registers.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This change is driven by social awareness within English, not foreign language influence.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Language adapts to evolving social consciousness—English adjusts its grammar to reflect inclusivity and recognition of diverse identities.\",\r\n      \"This represents linguistic evolution in response to social needs, not declining literacy.\",\r\n      \"Singular 'they' appears in both spoken and written registers; it's a deliberate grammatical adaptation, not confusion.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q673\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language borrowing and etymology\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The words piano, balcony, and opera entered English as a result of contact with which language?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"French\",\r\n      \"Italian\",\r\n      \"Spanish\",\r\n      \"German\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While French contributed many words to English, these specific terms are Italian.\",\r\n      \"Correct! These are Italian borrowings from the Renaissance era, reflecting Italy's influence on art and music.\",\r\n      \"Spanish contributed different vocabulary, particularly related to the Americas.\",\r\n      \"German contributed educational and philosophical terms, not these artistic ones.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q674\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language borrowing and etymology\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The English words cookie, boss, and yacht came into the language primarily through contact with speakers of:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Dutch\",\r\n      \"Gaelic\",\r\n      \"Portuguese\",\r\n      \"Norse\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Dutch traders and settlers contributed many everyday and nautical terms during the seventeenth century.\",\r\n      \"Gaelic contributed some place names and cultural terms but not these specific words.\",\r\n      \"Portuguese contributed fewer terms to English, mainly related to trade.\",\r\n      \"Norse contributed many words but much earlier, during the Viking Age.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q675\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language borrowing and etymology\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The words canyon, patio, and rodeo were borrowed into English through interaction with:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Latin scholars in medieval England\",\r\n      \"Spanish colonists in North America\",\r\n      \"French explorers in Canada\",\r\n      \"Italian merchants in the Mediterranean\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While Latin influenced English scholarly vocabulary, these are modern Spanish borrowings.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Spanish influence spread throughout the Americas, leaving lasting imprints on English vocabulary.\",\r\n      \"French contributed Canadian terms but not these specifically Spanish words.\",\r\n      \"Italian merchants influenced trade vocabulary but not these western American terms.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q676\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language borrowing and etymology\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The words bungalow, pyjamas, and shampoo came into English through contact with which language?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Hindi\",\r\n      \"Greek\",\r\n      \"Swahili\",\r\n      \"Malay\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! These are borrowings from Hindi during British colonial contact with India.\",\r\n      \"Greek contributed scientific and philosophical terms, not these domestic words.\",\r\n      \"Swahili contributed some words through East African contact but not these specific terms.\",\r\n      \"Malay contributed some words but these are specifically from Hindi.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q677\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language borrowing and etymology\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The words kindergarten, doppelgänger, and waltz entered English as a result of cultural contact with:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"French artists\",\r\n      \"German speakers\",\r\n      \"Scandinavian traders\",\r\n      \"Celtic poets\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"French contributed many cultural terms but not these specifically German words.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Germany contributed many educational, philosophical, and cultural terms to English during the nineteenth century.\",\r\n      \"Scandinavian languages contributed earlier Norse vocabulary, not these German terms.\",\r\n      \"Celtic languages contributed place names and some cultural terms but not these German borrowings.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 1\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q678\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Misplaced and dangling modifiers\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following sentences contains a misplaced modifier?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"While driving to school, the fog made it difficult to see the road.\",\r\n      \"To improve her grades, Maria studied every night after dinner.\",\r\n      \"Running late, the students rushed to their next class.\",\r\n      \"The boy wearing a blue hat waved from across the street.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The fog wasn't driving to school; the modifier 'While driving to school' should modify the student, not the fog.\",\r\n      \"This correctly places the modifier—Maria is the one improving grades and studying.\",\r\n      \"This correctly places the modifier—the students are running late and rushing.\",\r\n      \"This correctly places the modifier—the boy is wearing the hat.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q679\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Misplaced and dangling modifiers\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Select the sentence that correctly places the modifier.\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Covered in sauce, the waiter served the pasta to the customer.\",\r\n      \"The waiter served the pasta covered in sauce to the customer.\",\r\n      \"The waiter covered the pasta in sauce before serving it to the customer.\",\r\n      \"Covered in sauce, the customer received the pasta from the waiter.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This misplaces the modifier—it suggests the waiter was covered in sauce.\",\r\n      \"This is ambiguous—it could mean the waiter or pasta is covered in sauce.\",\r\n      \"Correct! This version clearly shows that the pasta—not the waiter or the customer—was covered in sauce.\",\r\n      \"This misplaces the modifier—it suggests the customer was covered in sauce.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q680\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Misplaced and dangling modifiers\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which sentence contains a dangling modifier?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Hoping to earn a promotion, the employee worked late every night.\",\r\n      \"Walking to the store, the rain began to fall.\",\r\n      \"The teacher handed back the essays written by her students.\",\r\n      \"To prepare for the exam, Malik reviewed his notes carefully.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This correctly places the modifier—the employee is hoping and working.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'Walking to the store' illogically modifies 'the rain.' The implied subject (the walker) is missing.\",\r\n      \"This sentence has no dangling modifier—the modifier 'written by her students' correctly describes the essays.\",\r\n      \"This correctly places the modifier—Malik is preparing and reviewing.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q681\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Misplaced and dangling modifiers\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Choose the revision that eliminates the misplaced modifier. Original: She nearly watched the movie for three hours.\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"She watched the movie for nearly three hours.\",\r\n      \"Nearly for three hours, she watched the movie.\",\r\n      \"The movie she watched nearly for three hours.\",\r\n      \"She watched nearly the movie for three hours.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! 'Nearly' should modify the time spent, not the act of watching. This placement makes the meaning clear.\",\r\n      \"This awkward placement still creates ambiguity about what 'nearly' modifies.\",\r\n      \"This version is grammatically awkward and still misplaces 'nearly.'\",\r\n      \"This incorrectly places 'nearly' before 'the movie,' suggesting she almost watched the movie itself.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q682\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Misplaced and dangling modifiers\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following best illustrates a correct use of modifiers?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The teacher gave a test to the students that was difficult.\",\r\n      \"The teacher gave the students a difficult test.\",\r\n      \"The teacher gave a difficult test to the students who complained.\",\r\n      \"A test that was difficult the teacher gave to the students.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This misplaces 'that was difficult'—it could modify 'students' instead of 'test.'\",\r\n      \"Correct! The modifier 'difficult' is placed right before the noun it describes, avoiding ambiguity.\",\r\n      \"While grammatical, this changes meaning by adding 'who complained' rather than focusing on modifier placement.\",\r\n      \"This awkward word order violates standard English syntax.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q683\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Visual literacy and symbolic interpretation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A photograph shows a large, ornamental mirror hanging on a wall. The mirror's reflection is slightly distorted and shattered by a single, prominent crack that runs through the glass. In the foreground, a single, antique gavel (a judge's hammer) rests on a nearby desk. The visual image is most likely intended to suggest a cause-and-effect relationship between which of the following aspects of contemporary life?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"The cost of antique restoration and modern interior design.\",\r\n      \"The increasing pressure on legal professionals and the pursuit of political office.\",\r\n      \"A flawed justice system and the consequent breakdown of public trust.\",\r\n      \"The economic disparity between the wealthy and the working class.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While both are antiques, this interpretation misses the symbolic meaning of the specific objects chosen.\",\r\n      \"This interprets individual stress rather than the broader symbolic message about institutional integrity.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The gavel symbolizes the law and justice system (the cause). The cracked mirror symbolizes reflection and integrity that is shattered or fractured (the effect: loss of public faith/trust).\",\r\n      \"While gavels can represent authority, the cracked mirror specifically suggests broken reflection/trust, not economic class division.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q684\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Visual literacy and symbolic interpretation\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An illustration depicts a recognizable city skyline, half-submerged in water. In the foreground, an oversized, antique pocket watch has melted and warped over a tree branch, with its hands pointing toward a specific, recent year. The visual image is most likely intended to suggest a cause-and-effect relationship between which of the following aspects of contemporary life?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Historical shifts in fashion and industrial manufacturing practices.\",\r\n      \"The rapid passage of time and the effects of climate change.\",\r\n      \"The decline of physical timekeeping devices and the rise of digital technology.\",\r\n      \"The growth of maritime trade and the stability of coastal infrastructure.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The melted watch and submerged city are not related to fashion or manufacturing trends.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The melting watch/clock and the specific year suggest time is running out or change is accelerating (cause: time/inaction). The submerged city is the direct result (effect: flooding/climate catastrophe).\",\r\n      \"While the watch is antique, the submersion and melting suggest environmental urgency, not technological obsolescence.\",\r\n      \"This misreads the image—the submerged city indicates instability and crisis, not stable infrastructure.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q685\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Content-area literacy and instructional strategies\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A middle school science teacher assigns students to keep a reading journal while studying a unit on ecology. The most effective instructional purpose of this reading journal is to encourage students to:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Record predictions about what will happen next in the reading.\",\r\n      \"Focus on the emotional reactions and opinions the text generates.\",\r\n      \"Connect new science vocabulary to familiar terms and summarize key concepts.\",\r\n      \"Identify and critique the author's overall writing style and tone.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While predictions can support engagement, they don't directly address content mastery in informational science texts.\",\r\n      \"Emotional reactions are more appropriate for narrative texts; science texts require focus on concepts and vocabulary.\",\r\n      \"Correct! This directly addresses content learning (vocabulary, concepts, summarization) and active processing of informational text, making it the most effective use for this instructional goal.\",\r\n      \"Style and tone analysis are secondary to understanding ecological concepts and terminology in a science unit.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q686\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Metacognition and comprehension monitoring\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following reading journal prompts is most effective for promoting metacognition and deeper understanding of informational text content?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"List three facts from the chapter you think are the most interesting.\",\r\n      \"If you were the author, what would you title this chapter?\",\r\n      \"What specific sentence or paragraph confused you, and what steps did you take to try and clarify its meaning?\",\r\n      \"Write a short thank-you note to the author for providing this information.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Listing facts shows recall but doesn't promote reflection on the comprehension process itself.\",\r\n      \"Creating titles engages creativity but doesn't address monitoring one's own understanding of content.\",\r\n      \"Correct! This prompt forces students to monitor their own comprehension, identify breakdowns in understanding, and reflect on their problem-solving strategies—the core components of metacognition for content learning.\",\r\n      \"Thank-you notes are creative but don't develop metacognitive awareness of the reading process.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q687\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Assessment of content comprehension through journals\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A high school history teacher uses reading journal entries as part of a formal assessment. To best evaluate students' comprehension and retention of the content (historical events, dates, and causes), the teacher should prioritize journal entries that show the student can:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Use figurative language and creative illustrations to represent the reading.\",\r\n      \"Reflectively discuss how the historical events made them feel personally.\",\r\n      \"Formulate questions that connect the chapter's events to previously learned historical concepts.\",\r\n      \"Simply copy five direct quotes from the textbook without commentary.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Creative illustrations show engagement but don't necessarily demonstrate comprehension of historical content.\",\r\n      \"Personal reflection addresses emotional response rather than content mastery and retention.\",\r\n      \"Correct! This demonstrates higher-order thinking (analysis and synthesis) by requiring students to integrate new content with prior knowledge, which is a strong indicator of deep content learning and retention.\",\r\n      \"Copying quotes without analysis shows only the ability to locate information, not comprehension or retention.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q688\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Historical context and literary analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Students in an English class are reading a late nineteenth-century novel in which the unmarried female protagonist chooses not to leave her dependent living situation, despite inheriting money. The students are confused by her decision, arguing that she has the financial means to be independent. Which instructional activity would be most effective for helping students understand the protagonist's reluctance to strike out on her own?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Assign a research paper comparing the protagonist's inheritance to modern salary equivalents.\",\r\n      \"Discuss contemporary popular songs about seeking freedom and independence.\",\r\n      \"Lead a class debate on whether money truly brings happiness and independence.\",\r\n      \"Have students analyze excerpts from the historical 'Married Women's Property Acts' to understand the legal and credit restrictions placed on single women seeking to secure housing.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 3,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While understanding the value of money is relevant, it doesn't address the legal and social barriers that prevented independence.\",\r\n      \"Contemporary songs about freedom are anachronistic and don't provide historical context for 19th-century constraints.\",\r\n      \"A philosophical debate about money and happiness misses the specific historical restrictions the character faced.\",\r\n      \"Correct! This activity directly addresses the historical and legal constraints (inability to secure a loan, purchase property, or establish credit without a male relative) that are absent in the students' modern understanding. This primary source analysis is the most effective way to build historical empathy for the character's predicament.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q689\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Identity — \\\"Names\\\"\\n\\nThey gave me names before I spoke—\\nChild, student, daughter, friend.\\nEach name fit once, then cracked with time,\\nUntil I shaped my own again.\\n\\nWhich theme is best reflected in the poem?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Identity changes as individuals grow and define themselves.\",\r\n      \"Family and community determine who a person becomes.\",\r\n      \"Social titles are meaningless labels.\",\r\n      \"Childhood is the truest stage of selfhood.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The imagery of names 'cracking with time' shows self-definition through personal evolution.\",\r\n      \"The poem emphasizes personal agency over external determination.\",\r\n      \"The poem acknowledges the significance of names, not their meaninglessness.\",\r\n      \"The poem suggests growth beyond childhood, not that childhood is truest.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q690\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Religious Differences — \\\"Two Altars\\\"\\n\\nIn one small town, two altars stand,\\nEach built from hope and stone.\\nThe hymns rise up in different tongues,\\nBut reach one God alone.\\n\\nWhat message does the poem most clearly convey?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Religious devotion requires shared language.\",\r\n      \"True worship unites believers despite differences.\",\r\n      \"One religion must replace another for peace.\",\r\n      \"Separate rituals lead to separate gods.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The poem emphasizes unity, not the necessity of shared language.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Parallel imagery of 'two altars' and 'one God' highlights unity through faith diversity.\",\r\n      \"The poem celebrates coexistence, not replacement.\",\r\n      \"The poem explicitly states they reach 'one God alone,' contradicting this option.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q691\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Spiritual Enlightenment — \\\"The Lantern\\\"\\n\\nI carried lanterns through the night,\\nEach promised me the way.\\nTheir flames grew dim, their wicks burned out—\\nThen dawn became my day.\\n\\nWhich interpretation best fits the poem's central theme?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Spiritual understanding often appears after false pursuits fade.\",\r\n      \"Knowledge is found only through external study.\",\r\n      \"Faith depends on tradition and ritual.\",\r\n      \"The search for truth leads mostly to disappointment.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The extinguished lanterns represent misguided searches; dawn symbolizes internal awakening.\",\r\n      \"The poem suggests internal discovery (dawn) rather than external study (lanterns).\",\r\n      \"The poem focuses on personal enlightenment, not tradition.\",\r\n      \"The poem ends with enlightenment (dawn), not disappointment.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q692\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Romantic Love — \\\"After the Storm\\\"\\n\\nThe storm has passed, the branches bend,\\nThe air is sharp and clean.\\nYour laughter drifts across the yard—\\nA quiet proof of what love means.\\n\\nWhat theme does the imagery in this poem suggest?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Love endures through hardship and renewal.\",\r\n      \"Nature reflects the destruction caused by love.\",\r\n      \"Passion fades when calm returns.\",\r\n      \"Love exists only in peaceful moments.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The storm symbolizes conflict; the calm and laughter show resilience and restoration.\",\r\n      \"The poem shows renewal, not destruction.\",\r\n      \"The laughter after the storm shows love continuing, not fading.\",\r\n      \"The poem explicitly mentions a storm, showing love exists through challenges.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q693\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Theme: Identity — \\\"Mirror\\\"\\n\\nI look into the mirror's face,\\nBut see my mother's eyes.\\nHer voice still echoes in my thoughts,\\nYet now my own replies.\\n\\nWhich statement best expresses the theme of the poem?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Family traits shape but do not define personal identity.\",\r\n      \"Parents always influence their children's choices.\",\r\n      \"People never escape the past.\",\r\n      \"Mirrors can reveal hidden truths.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The speaker sees resemblance to her mother but also hears her own emerging voice — independence within connection.\",\r\n      \"The poem shows influence but also personal agency ('my own replies').\",\r\n      \"The poem shows evolution beyond the past, not entrapment.\",\r\n      \"This is too literal; the theme is about identity, not mirrors.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q694\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Theme: Religious Differences — \\\"Two Doors\\\"\\n\\nHe prays at dawn; I pray at noon.\\nOur songs rise up the same.\\nDifferent doors, the same bright sky—\\nWe call one light by name.\\n\\nWhat is the main idea of the poem?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Religious differences prevent unity.\",\r\n      \"True faith is found through strict ritual.\",\r\n      \"People of different faiths can share spiritual connection.\",\r\n      \"Worship should occur at the same time for everyone.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The poem emphasizes unity ('same bright sky'), not division.\",\r\n      \"The poem celebrates flexibility in practice, not strict ritual.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Both worshippers use different forms but reach toward the same spiritual truth.\",\r\n      \"The poem explicitly shows different times (dawn vs. noon) as acceptable.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q695\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Theme: Spiritual Enlightenment — \\\"The Quiet Answer\\\"\\n\\nI asked the stars for meaning,\\nBut silence filled the air.\\nThen, in that hush, my heart replied—\\nThe truth was always there.\\n\\nWhich theme best fits this poem?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Enlightenment comes through quiet inner reflection.\",\r\n      \"Wisdom is gained only from teachers.\",\r\n      \"Life's mysteries are impossible to solve.\",\r\n      \"The universe answers all human questions.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The speaker finds insight not from the outside world, but from inner awareness.\",\r\n      \"The poem emphasizes self-discovery, not external teaching.\",\r\n      \"The poem shows answers are found, not that mysteries are impossible to solve.\",\r\n      \"The stars are silent; the answer comes from within, not the universe.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q696\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Theme: Romantic Love — \\\"Hands\\\"\\n\\nYour hand found mine in crowded streets,\\nNo word, no grand display—\\nJust quiet proof that love can bloom\\nIn ordinary day.\\n\\nWhat central idea is expressed in the poem?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"True love is dramatic and public.\",\r\n      \"Love reveals itself through small, gentle acts.\",\r\n      \"Romance must always defy the ordinary.\",\r\n      \"Words are the strongest sign of love.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The poem explicitly rejects grand display, emphasizing quiet gestures.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Love is shown through quiet gestures, not spectacle.\",\r\n      \"The poem celebrates the ordinary, not defiance of it.\",\r\n      \"The poem states 'No word,' showing actions over words.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q697\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Title: The Alchemist (1988)\\nAuthor: Paulo Coelho\\n\\nSantiago, a young shepherd in Spain, keeps dreaming about treasure buried near the Egyptian pyramids. Against everyone's advice, he sells his flock and begins a long, punishing journey across the desert. He's robbed, betrayed, and tested by failure, but he keeps moving forward—guided by faith in his Personal Legend (his unique purpose in life).\\n\\nThrough persistence, he learns that the \\\"treasure\\\" he sought wasn't gold, but wisdom and self-knowledge gained from the journey itself.\\n\\nWhich statement best expresses a central theme of The Alchemist?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Destiny rewards only those born into privilege.\",\r\n      \"Perseverance and faith transform obstacles into opportunities.\",\r\n      \"Achieving dreams requires abandoning spiritual belief.\",\r\n      \"True success depends solely on external circumstances.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"The text shows Santiago's journey transcends privilege; his wealth is sold and lost.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Santiago's relentless pursuit of his dream and his trust in the journey illustrate that perseverance and belief lead to transformation.\",\r\n      \"Santiago's spiritual faith guides him throughout; it's central, not abandoned.\",\r\n      \"Santiago's transformation comes from internal growth and persistence, not external factors.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q698\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Theme Analysis\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Title: A Raisin in the Sun\\nAuthor: Lorraine Hansberry\\nTheme: The perseverance needed to achieve a personal dream\\n\\nWalter Lee Younger paces the small apartment, his voice tight with frustration.\\n\\n\\\"Sometimes it feels like all I do is work for other people's dreams,\\\" he says.\\nRuth watches quietly, weary but determined.\\n\\\"Then work for your own,\\\" she answers. \\\"Just don't forget the rest of us while you're at it.\\\"\\nHe stops. The worn linoleum gleams faintly in the morning light.\\n\\\"I ain't quitting,\\\" he says. \\\"I just want something that's mine.\\\"\\n\\nIn this moment, Walter faces the weight of deferred hope—but he also refuses to surrender it.\\n\\nWhich statement best expresses a central idea of this passage?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Family obligations make personal dreams impossible to achieve.\",\r\n      \"The determination to rise above hardship gives life its dignity.\",\r\n      \"Material wealth is the only true measure of success.\",\r\n      \"Conflict within families destroys ambition.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Ruth encourages Walter to pursue his dreams while maintaining family bonds, not abandon them.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Walter's persistence, even amid poverty and frustration, illustrates that dignity and meaning come from perseverance in pursuit of one's dream.\",\r\n      \"Walter seeks something meaningful and personal ('mine'), not just material wealth.\",\r\n      \"The passage shows Ruth supporting Walter's ambition, not conflict destroying it.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 3\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q699\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Parallel Structure and Language Conventions\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Many people choose to start the day with healthy routines.\\nThey might go for a brisk walk, prepare a balanced breakfast, and read something uplifting before work.\\nThese habits can boost energy, sharpen focus, and improve mood throughout the morning.\\nTo make these practices most effective, a person should plan ahead, stay consistent, and __________.\\n\\nWhich sentence best maintains the parallel structure of the paragraph?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"try to keep distractions at a minimum\",\r\n      \"maintaining a positive mindset\",\r\n      \"they will probably feel better all day\",\r\n      \"making sure to get enough rest\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! 'Plan ahead, stay consistent, and try to keep distractions at a minimum' keeps all three verbs in the same base-verb form ('plan,' 'stay,' 'try'). The others break the pattern or shift tense.\",\r\n      \"This uses a gerund ('maintaining') which breaks the parallel structure with the base verbs.\",\r\n      \"This shifts to a future tense clause and changes the subject, breaking parallelism.\",\r\n      \"This uses a gerund ('making sure') which doesn't match the base verb pattern.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q700\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Transitions and Cohesiveness\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Many cities are investing in green roofs—gardens planted on top of buildings.\\nThese spaces help insulate structures, reduce stormwater runoff, and provide habitats for birds and insects.\\n__________, they also offer residents peaceful areas for relaxation and community gardening.\\n\\nWhich transition best maintains the cohesiveness of the excerpt?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"However\",\r\n      \"For example\",\r\n      \"Moreover\",\r\n      \"On the contrary\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"'However' signals contrast, which would contradict the positive benefits already listed.\",\r\n      \"'For example' suggests a specific instance, but this sentence adds another benefit, not an example.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'Moreover' adds information in support of the previous idea, linking environmental benefits with social ones.\",\r\n      \"'On the contrary' introduces opposition, which doesn't fit the supportive tone.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q701\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Transitions and Cohesiveness\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"The city recently expanded its public transportation system to reduce traffic congestion.\\nRidership has increased significantly, and downtown air quality has improved.\\n__________, suburban commuters still prefer to drive their own vehicles.\\n\\nWhich transition best completes the excerpt?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"However\",\r\n      \"For example\",\r\n      \"Moreover\",\r\n      \"In addition\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! 'However' signals contrast — despite improvements, some people continue to drive.\",\r\n      \"'For example' would suggest an illustration, not a contrast.\",\r\n      \"'Moreover' would add supporting information, not contrast.\",\r\n      \"'In addition' would continue the positive trend, not introduce a contrasting fact.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q702\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Transitions and Cohesiveness\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Many schools encourage students to participate in community service projects.\\nThese activities help young people develop empathy and responsibility.\\n__________, students might volunteer at a food bank or assist at a local shelter.\\n\\nWhich transition best completes the excerpt?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"For example\",\r\n      \"On the contrary\",\r\n      \"Moreover\",\r\n      \"However\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The third sentence provides a specific illustration of the general idea in the previous one.\",\r\n      \"'On the contrary' would contradict the previous statement, which doesn't fit here.\",\r\n      \"'Moreover' would add another benefit, but this sentence provides examples, not additional benefits.\",\r\n      \"'However' would introduce contrast, which doesn't match the supporting examples given.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q703\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Transitions and Cohesiveness\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Exercise improves physical health by strengthening the heart and muscles.\\n__________, it can also enhance mental well-being by reducing stress and anxiety.\\n\\nWhich transition best completes the excerpt?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"However\",\r\n      \"For example\",\r\n      \"Moreover\",\r\n      \"On the contrary\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"'However' would introduce contrast, but the sentence adds supporting information.\",\r\n      \"'For example' would suggest a specific instance, not an additional benefit.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'Moreover' adds a reinforcing benefit to the initial claim.\",\r\n      \"'On the contrary' would contradict the first statement, which doesn't fit the supportive tone.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q704\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Transitions and Cohesiveness\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Some people believe technology isolates individuals from one another.\\n__________, many online communities bring people together and foster genuine friendships.\\n\\nWhich transition best completes the excerpt?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Moreover\",\r\n      \"On the contrary\",\r\n      \"For example\",\r\n      \"In addition\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"'Moreover' would add support to the first idea, but this sentence refutes it.\",\r\n      \"Correct! 'On the contrary' directly refutes the idea expressed in the first sentence.\",\r\n      \"'For example' would provide an illustration supporting the first sentence, not refuting it.\",\r\n      \"'In addition' would continue supporting the first idea, not contradicting it.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q705\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Annotating a text would be the most effective reading strategy to use for which of the following purposes?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Estimating the time it will take to read an entire chapter.\",\r\n      \"Generating an initial list of potential topics for a final research paper.\",\r\n      \"Tracking the development of a complex character's motivations throughout a novel.\",\r\n      \"Determining the author's primary source material used to write the historical non-fiction book.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Time estimation requires skimming or scanning, not detailed annotation.\",\r\n      \"Topic generation is better served by brainstorming or freewriting, not annotation.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Annotating (taking notes directly in the margins, highlighting, and asking questions) is a close reading strategy. It is essential for tracking complex, subtle changes in character, theme, or argument over a long period, which cannot be accomplished through superficial reading.\",\r\n      \"Identifying primary sources requires examining citations and references, not annotation of the text itself.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q706\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Skills & Processes\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Reading Strategies\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Summarizing a lengthy historical document would be the most effective reading strategy to use for which of the following purposes?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Locating specific evidence needed to support a narrow claim in an essay.\",\r\n      \"Identifying the main arguments and conclusions to ensure overall comprehension before class discussion.\",\r\n      \"Evaluating the syntax and precise word choice used by the author.\",\r\n      \"Connecting new terminology to personal prior knowledge and experiences.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Locating specific evidence requires scanning or close reading, not summarizing.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Summarizing is a strategy aimed at distilling the core message by identifying only the main ideas and supporting claims, thus ensuring the student grasps the overall comprehension (gist) of the document without getting lost in the details.\",\r\n      \"Evaluating syntax and word choice requires close reading and analysis, not summarizing.\",\r\n      \"Connecting terminology to prior knowledge requires elaboration or personal reflection strategies, not summarizing.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q707\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Expository Writing\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A student is revising the introduction to an expository essay explaining how to manage test anxiety.\\nWhich of the following versions of the opening sentence would be most appropriate for the essay?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Many students hate taking tests because they make people nervous and tired.\",\r\n      \"Tests can cause anxiety, but a few simple strategies can make the experience calmer and more productive.\",\r\n      \"Test anxiety has always been around and probably always will be.\",\r\n      \"Everyone knows tests are stressful, so this paper will talk about that.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This is too informal and vague, lacking the clear purpose and tone expected in expository writing.\",\r\n      \"Correct! It clearly introduces the topic (test anxiety) and sets up an explanatory purpose (offering strategies), matching the expository task.\",\r\n      \"This is too general and doesn't establish the essay's purpose or direction.\",\r\n      \"This uses informal language ('everyone knows', 'talk about') and doesn't clearly preview the essay's content.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q708\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Expository Writing\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A student is writing an expository essay describing how to save money on groceries.\\nWhich of the following topic sentences would be most appropriate for the first paragraph?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Groceries are very expensive these days, which is really annoying.\",\r\n      \"Shopping for food can take a long time if you are not organized.\",\r\n      \"Saving money on groceries is possible when shoppers plan meals, use coupons, and compare prices.\",\r\n      \"Everyone has to eat, but not everyone enjoys grocery shopping.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This is too informal and expresses opinion rather than establishing an expository focus.\",\r\n      \"This shifts focus to time management rather than the essay's stated topic of saving money.\",\r\n      \"Correct! This topic sentence clearly previews the paragraph's main idea and provides a roadmap of specific strategies (planning, coupons, comparing prices) that align with the expository purpose.\",\r\n      \"This is too general and doesn't address the essay's specific focus on saving money.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q709\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Organizational Structures\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A student is writing an expository essay explaining how the invention of the printing press influenced literacy rates in Europe.\\nWhich organizational structure would be most effective?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Sequential – listing inventions before and after the printing press\",\r\n      \"Cause and Effect – showing how the invention led to rising literacy\",\r\n      \"Problem and Solution – describing the lack of books, then offering printing as the fix\",\r\n      \"Spatial – describing printing shops in various cities\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Sequential structure focuses on chronological order, not the relationship between the invention and its impact.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The essay explains how one development (printing press) produced another outcome (rising literacy). Cause and Effect is the most effective structure.\",\r\n      \"While this could work, the essay's focus is on influence (cause and effect), not presenting a problem that needs solving.\",\r\n      \"Spatial structure organizes by location, which doesn't address the causal relationship the essay explores.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q710\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Organizational Structures\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A student plans to describe how water travels through the human body, from ingestion to elimination.\\nWhich structure should the student use?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Sequential\",\r\n      \"Problem and Solution\",\r\n      \"Compare and Contrast\",\r\n      \"Cause and Effect\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The explanation follows a process in time order, making Sequential the most appropriate structure.\",\r\n      \"Problem and Solution addresses issues and remedies, not a step-by-step process.\",\r\n      \"Compare and Contrast analyzes similarities and differences, which doesn't fit a process description.\",\r\n      \"While cause and effect might show relationships, the primary focus is on the sequence of steps.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q711\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Organizational Structures\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A student wants to write about air pollution in major U.S. cities and propose ways to reduce it.\\nWhich organizational pattern would best suit this purpose?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Spatial – grouping information by location\",\r\n      \"Problem and Solution – describing pollution issues and offering remedies\",\r\n      \"Chronological – explaining pollution through time\",\r\n      \"Cause and Effect – focusing on why pollution exists\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"While spatial organization could work for organizing by city, it doesn't address the core purpose of proposing solutions.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The task involves presenting an issue (air pollution) and recommending fixes (solutions). Problem and Solution is the best fit.\",\r\n      \"Chronological structure focuses on time sequence, not problem-solving.\",\r\n      \"Cause and Effect explains why pollution exists but doesn't emphasize the solutions aspect of the essay.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q712\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Genre & Craft\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Organizational Structures\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A student is creating an essay comparing the architecture of northern and southern Europe.\\nWhich structure is most effective?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Cause and Effect\",\r\n      \"Problem and Solution\",\r\n      \"Compare and Contrast\",\r\n      \"Sequential\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Cause and Effect shows relationships between events, not similarities and differences.\",\r\n      \"Problem and Solution addresses issues and remedies, not comparative analysis.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The essay centers on analyzing similarities and differences between two subjects (northern and southern European architecture).\",\r\n      \"Sequential structure follows chronological or step-by-step order, not comparison.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q713\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Word Relationships - Cognates\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which pair of words are true cognates?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Library / librería\",\r\n      \"Animal / animal\",\r\n      \"Rope / ropa\",\r\n      \"Actual / actualmente\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"False cognate. Library (English) means a place for books, but librería (Spanish) means bookstore.\",\r\n      \"Correct! They share both meaning and origin (Latin animalis). Both mean the same thing in English and Spanish.\",\r\n      \"False cognate. Rope (English) means cord, but ropa (Spanish) means clothing.\",\r\n      \"False cognate. Actual (English) means real/current, but actualmente (Spanish) means currently/nowadays.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q714\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Word Relationships - Cognates\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A teacher explains that attention (English) and atención (Spanish) have the same Latin root.\\nWhat skill is the teacher promoting?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Morphological awareness\",\r\n      \"Syntax development\",\r\n      \"Cognate recognition\",\r\n      \"Phonemic segmentation\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Morphological awareness involves understanding word parts (prefixes, suffixes, roots), not cross-language connections.\",\r\n      \"Syntax development focuses on sentence structure, not vocabulary relationships.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The teacher is helping students recognize cognates—words that share origins and meanings across languages.\",\r\n      \"Phonemic segmentation involves breaking words into individual sounds, not recognizing cross-language word relationships.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q715\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Word Relationships - Cognates\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"An English learner confuses sensible (English) with sensible (Spanish) meaning 'sensitive.'\\nThis confusion illustrates which concept?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Homophone interference\",\r\n      \"False cognates\",\r\n      \"Semantic drift\",\r\n      \"Syntax transfer\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Homophones are words that sound the same but have different meanings within one language, not across languages.\",\r\n      \"Correct! False cognates are words that look similar in two languages but have different meanings. Sensible (English) means reasonable, while sensible (Spanish) means sensitive.\",\r\n      \"Semantic drift refers to how word meanings change over time within a language, not cross-language confusion.\",\r\n      \"Syntax transfer involves applying sentence structure rules from one language to another, not vocabulary confusion.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q716\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Evolution and Archaic Words\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which of the following best defines an archaic English word?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"A slang term newly added to the dictionary\",\r\n      \"A word that has fallen out of common use but appears in older texts\",\r\n      \"A word used mainly in technology fields\",\r\n      \"A misspelled modern word\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Slang terms are contemporary, not archaic. Archaic words are from the past.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Archaic words are those that were once common but are no longer in everyday use, like thee, thou, and wherefore.\",\r\n      \"Technical terms are specialized modern vocabulary, not archaic words.\",\r\n      \"Archaic words are legitimate historical terms, not spelling errors.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q717\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Evolution and Phonetic Spelling\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which example shows a phonetic spelling of an archaic English word?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"Knight → nite\",\r\n      \"Thou → you\",\r\n      \"Ye olde shoppe → shop\",\r\n      \"Brunch → breakfast + lunch\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! 'Nite' represents a phonetic modernization of 'knight' — same sound, modernized spelling form.\",\r\n      \"This is word replacement/evolution, not phonetic spelling of the same word.\",\r\n      \"This is a stylized archaic spelling, not a phonetic modernization.\",\r\n      \"This is a blend/portmanteau, not phonetic spelling of an archaic word.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q718\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Meaning & Communication\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Language Evolution and New Words\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Which factor most increases the likelihood that a new word will enter Standard American English?\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"It expresses a widely needed concept or object\",\r\n      \"It is used only by teenagers online\",\r\n      \"It has complicated spelling and no clear meaning\",\r\n      \"It originates as a private joke on TV\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! Words that fill communication needs (like brunch, smog, podcast) are most likely to be adopted into standard usage.\",\r\n      \"Limited demographic use reduces the likelihood of mainstream adoption.\",\r\n      \"Complexity and unclear meaning hinder adoption, not help it.\",\r\n      \"Limited context and audience reduce the chances of widespread acceptance.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q719\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Cultural and Social Contexts\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A small group within a larger society that shares common values, behaviors, or language patterns is known as a:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"ethnic group\",\r\n      \"speech community\",\r\n      \"microculture\",\r\n      \"acculturated group\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Ethnic groups are connected by shared ancestry and heritage, not necessarily small subgroups.\",\r\n      \"Speech communities share language and communication rules, but may not be small groups within a larger society.\",\r\n      \"Correct! A microculture is any small social group—such as bilingual students—bound by shared traits within a dominant culture.\",\r\n      \"Acculturated groups adopt traits from dominant culture while maintaining their own identity, not necessarily small subgroups.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q720\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Cultural and Social Contexts\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"A group of people connected by shared ancestry, history, or cultural heritage is referred to as a:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"ethnic group\",\r\n      \"microculture\",\r\n      \"acculturated group\",\r\n      \"speech community\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! An ethnic group is defined by shared ancestry, history, and cultural heritage.\",\r\n      \"Microculture refers to small subgroups within a larger society, not necessarily based on ancestry.\",\r\n      \"Acculturated groups adopt traits from another culture while maintaining their own, not primarily defined by ancestry.\",\r\n      \"Speech communities are defined by shared language use, not ancestry or heritage.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q721\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Cultural and Social Contexts\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Individuals who share a common language and rules for its use within a broader society are known as a:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"microculture\",\r\n      \"speech community\",\r\n      \"acculturated group\",\r\n      \"ethnic group\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Microculture refers to small groups with shared values/behaviors, not specifically language-based.\",\r\n      \"Correct! A speech community is defined by shared language and linguistic norms within a broader society.\",\r\n      \"Acculturated groups adopt cultural traits, not necessarily defined by shared language rules.\",\r\n      \"Ethnic groups are defined by ancestry and heritage, not specifically by shared language use.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q722\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Cultural and Social Contexts\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"When a group adopts traits from a dominant culture while maintaining aspects of its own identity, it is considered an:\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"ethnic group\",\r\n      \"speech community\",\r\n      \"acculturated group\",\r\n      \"microculture\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 2,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Ethnic groups are defined by shared heritage, not necessarily by cultural adaptation.\",\r\n      \"Speech communities are defined by shared language use, not cultural adaptation.\",\r\n      \"Correct! An acculturated group adopts traits from a dominant culture while maintaining aspects of its own identity. This involves cultural adaptation without complete assimilation.\",\r\n      \"Microculture refers to small subgroups within a larger society, not specifically about cultural adaptation.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q723\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Contrastive Intonation and Stress\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Use the sentence fragment below to answer the question that follows.\\n\\nI didn't finish grading the essays, but I certainly can _______.\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"throw them away and start over\",\r\n      \"finish them tomorrow morning\",\r\n      \"ask a student to grade for me\",\r\n      \"tell the class I'm done anyway\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This doesn't solve the original task and abandons the goal.\",\r\n      \"Correct! The stress on 'can' signals capability after limitation — can't finish now, but can soon.\",\r\n      \"This avoids the responsibility rather than addressing the capability.\",\r\n      \"This is dishonest and doesn't reflect the 'can' capability being emphasized.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q724\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Contrastive Intonation and Stress\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Use the sentence fragment below to answer the question that follows.\\n\\nI couldn't join the meeting in person, but I certainly can _______.\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"write the minutes afterward\",\r\n      \"log in remotely to participate\",\r\n      \"skip the meeting next week\",\r\n      \"apologize to the organizer\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"This is a different task, not maintaining the goal of participation.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Keeps the same goal (participation) while solving the limitation.\",\r\n      \"This abandons the goal rather than finding an alternative.\",\r\n      \"This doesn't solve the participation problem.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q725\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Contrastive Intonation and Stress\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Use the sentence fragment below to answer the question that follows.\\n\\nI didn't bring lunch today, but I certainly can _______.\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"grab something from the cafeteria\",\r\n      \"skip lunch altogether\",\r\n      \"borrow someone's meal\",\r\n      \"wait until dinner\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The contrastive intonation highlights a quick, realistic solution.\",\r\n      \"This avoids solving the problem rather than addressing it.\",\r\n      \"This is unrealistic and doesn't reflect self-efficacy.\",\r\n      \"This delays rather than solves the immediate need.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q726\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Contrastive Intonation and Stress\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Use the sentence fragment below to answer the question that follows.\\n\\nI can't afford a new car right now, but I certainly can _______.\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"complain about gas prices\",\r\n      \"save up and budget each month\",\r\n      \"ignore my old car's problems\",\r\n      \"borrow a luxury car for fun\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 1,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Complaining doesn't solve the affordability issue.\",\r\n      \"Correct! Fits the 'can't X but can Y to fix X' rhythm of confident contrast.\",\r\n      \"Ignoring problems doesn't address the capability being emphasized.\",\r\n      \"This is unrealistic and doesn't work toward the goal.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n    \"id\": \"Q727\",\r\n    \"subarea\": \"Literature & Understanding\",\r\n    \"objective\": \"Contrastive Intonation and Stress\",\r\n    \"stem\": \"Use the sentence fragment below to answer the question that follows.\\n\\nI didn't understand the lesson at first, but I certainly can _______.\",\r\n    \"options\": [\r\n      \"review the notes and ask questions\",\r\n      \"pretend I already knew it\",\r\n      \"complain about the teacher\",\r\n      \"drop the class altogether\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"correctIndex\": 0,\r\n    \"rationales\": [\r\n      \"Correct! The falling intonation after 'can' emphasizes self-efficacy — a positive alternative action.\",\r\n      \"This avoids addressing the understanding gap.\",\r\n      \"This doesn't solve the comprehension issue.\",\r\n      \"This abandons the learning goal rather than pursuing it.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"difficulty\": 2\r\n  }\r\n]\r\n;\n});","lineCount":5774,"map":[[5774,3]],"functionMap":null},"type":"js/module"}]}